window.PGET_EXAM = {"id":"obgyn","title":"PGET Paper II — Obstetrics & Gynaecology","short":"Paper II · Obs & Gynae","subtitle":"Mock exam built on the UHS Table of Specifications for Obstetrics & Gynaecology","durationMin":120,"questions":[{"id":1,"subject":"Anatomy of Reproductive and Genital Tract","topic":"Female Reproductive Anatomy","domain":"knowledge","question":"During a total abdominal hysterectomy, the surgeon clamps the uterine vessels at the level of the internal os. Which structure is at greatest risk because it passes immediately beneath the uterine artery about 1.5–2 cm lateral to the supravaginal cervix?","options":["Round ligament of the uterus","Ureter","Internal iliac vein","Vaginal artery","Obturator nerve"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In the base of the broad ligament (cardinal ligament) the ureter runs forwards beneath the uterine artery about 1.5–2 cm lateral to the cervix, then passes close to the lateral vaginal fornix to reach the bladder. Mnemonic: 'water under the bridge'. This is the commonest site of ureteric injury at hysterectomy, together with the infundibulopelvic ligament.","why_wrong":{"4":"The obturator nerve runs along the lateral pelvic wall towards the obturator canal, well away from the cervix.","2":"The internal iliac vein lies on the posterolateral pelvic wall, not under the uterine artery beside the cervix.","0":"The round ligament runs from the uterine cornu through the deep inguinal ring, far above the level of the internal os.","3":"The vaginal artery supplies the vagina and is not the classic structure crossed by the uterine artery at this point."}},{"id":2,"subject":"Anatomy of Reproductive and Genital Tract","topic":"Female Reproductive Anatomy","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 56-year-old woman undergoes staging laparotomy for an epithelial ovarian carcinoma. Lymphatics travelling with the ovarian vessels in the infundibulopelvic ligament drain primarily to which nodal group?","options":["Sacral nodes","Para-aortic (lumbar) nodes","Superficial inguinal nodes","Deep inguinal nodes","External iliac nodes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The ovary develops on the posterior abdominal wall and descends, carrying its vessels and lymphatics with it; ovarian lymph follows the ovarian vessels to the para-aortic (lumbar) nodes at about the level of the renal vessels (L1–L2). This is why para-aortic node sampling is part of ovarian cancer staging. (Some drainage also reaches pelvic nodes via the broad ligament, but the principal route is para-aortic.)","why_wrong":{"2":"Superficial inguinal nodes drain the vulva, lower vagina and, via the round ligament, a small area near the uterine cornu.","4":"External iliac nodes mainly drain the cervix and upper vagina, not the ovary via its vessels.","0":"Sacral nodes receive some cervical and rectal lymph, not the ovarian vessel lymphatics.","3":"Deep inguinal nodes receive lymph from the superficial inguinal nodes and the lower limb, not the ovary."}},{"id":3,"subject":"Anatomy of Reproductive and Genital Tract","topic":"Female Reproductive Anatomy","domain":"knowledge","question":"A house officer assists with a transvaginal pudendal nerve block before an outlet forceps delivery. Which bony landmark is palpated to guide the needle so that local anaesthetic is deposited just behind the sacrospinous ligament?","options":["Tip of the coccyx","Sacral promontory","Ischial spine","Ischial tuberosity","Pubic tubercle"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The pudendal nerve (S2–S4) leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen, crosses the sacrospinous ligament just medial to the ischial spine and enters the perineum via the lesser sciatic foramen. In a pudendal block the ischial spine is palpated vaginally and the needle is passed through the sacrospinous ligament just medial and posterior to it.","why_wrong":{"3":"The ischial tuberosity is used for the perineal (transperineal) approach landmarks, not the transvaginal block, and lies below the nerve's crossing point.","1":"The sacral promontory is used to measure the diagonal conjugate, not for pudendal block.","0":"The coccyx tip is far posterior to where the nerve crosses the sacrospinous ligament.","4":"The pubic tubercle is an anterior landmark used for the inguinal ligament and femoral hernia, unrelated to the pudendal nerve."}},{"id":4,"subject":"Anatomy of Reproductive and Genital Tract","topic":"Female Pelvis","domain":"knowledge","question":"On pelvic assessment of a primigravida, the diagonal conjugate is measured as 12.5 cm. Subtracting 1.5–2 cm gives an estimate of which diameter, the shortest anteroposterior diameter of the inlet through which the fetal head must pass?","options":["Obstetric conjugate","Interspinous diameter","Transverse diameter of the inlet","Anteroposterior diameter of the outlet","Anatomical (true) conjugate"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The obstetric conjugate runs from the sacral promontory to the nearest point on the posterior surface of the symphysis pubis (about 10.5–11 cm) and is the shortest AP diameter of the inlet. It cannot be measured directly, so it is estimated by subtracting 1.5–2 cm from the clinically measured diagonal conjugate (promontory to lower border of symphysis).","why_wrong":{"4":"The true (anatomical) conjugate is measured to the upper border of the symphysis and is slightly longer (about 11 cm) than the obstetric conjugate.","1":"The interspinous diameter is a transverse midpelvic measurement (about 10 cm), not an inlet AP diameter.","2":"The transverse diameter of the inlet (about 13 cm) is the widest inlet diameter, not an AP one.","3":"The AP diameter of the outlet runs from the lower symphysis to the sacrococcygeal joint/coccyx tip and is not derived from the diagonal conjugate."}},{"id":5,"subject":"Anatomy of Reproductive and Genital Tract","topic":"Female Pelvis","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 26-year-old woman with a suspected ruptured ectopic pregnancy has free fluid on ultrasound. Blood in the most dependent part of her peritoneal cavity can be aspirated by inserting a needle through which site?","options":["Ischiorectal fossa","Perineal body","Lateral vaginal fornix","Anterior vaginal fornix","Posterior vaginal fornix"],"answer":4,"explanation":"The rectouterine pouch (pouch of Douglas) is the lowest part of the female peritoneal cavity and lies directly behind the posterior vaginal fornix, separated from it only by the vaginal wall and peritoneum. Culdocentesis is therefore done through the posterior fornix; it is also the route for draining a pelvic abscess.","why_wrong":{"3":"The anterior fornix is related to the vesicouterine pouch and bladder, not the most dependent peritoneal recess.","2":"The lateral fornix is related to the ureter and uterine vessels, making it unsafe and not related to the pouch of Douglas.","1":"The perineal body is a fibromuscular node below the pelvic floor, outside the peritoneal cavity.","0":"The ischiorectal fossa lies below the levator ani and is extraperitoneal."}},{"id":6,"subject":"Anatomy of Reproductive and Genital Tract","topic":"Embryology","domain":"knowledge","question":"In female embryological development, the uterus, uterine tubes and upper part of the vagina arise from the paramesonephric ducts. The lower part of the vagina, formed from the vaginal plate (sinovaginal bulbs), is derived from which structure?","options":["Genital tubercle","Urogenital sinus","Mesonephric duct","Paramesonephric duct","Urorectal septum"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The vagina has a dual origin: the upper portion from the uterine canal (fused paramesonephric ducts) and the lower portion from the vaginal plate, which develops from the sinovaginal bulbs of the urogenital sinus. Failure of canalisation of the vaginal plate or of its junction with the Müllerian part underlies transverse vaginal septa, and the hymen marks the junction with the urogenital sinus.","why_wrong":{"2":"The mesonephric duct largely regresses in females, leaving vestigial remnants such as Gartner's duct and the epoophoron.","0":"The genital tubercle forms the clitoris, not the vagina.","3":"The paramesonephric ducts form the upper vagina, uterus and tubes, not the lower vagina.","4":"The urorectal septum divides the cloaca into rectum and urogenital sinus and forms the perineal body, not vaginal epithelium."}},{"id":7,"subject":"Anatomy of Reproductive and Genital Tract","topic":"Embryology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 30-year-old woman has a smooth, painless 2 cm cystic swelling on the anterolateral wall of the upper vagina found at a routine speculum examination. Histology after excision shows a cyst lined by cuboidal epithelium. This cyst is a remnant of which embryological structure?","options":["Mesonephric (Wolffian) duct","Paraurethral (Skene) gland","Paramesonephric (Müllerian) duct","Greater vestibular (Bartholin) gland","Gubernaculum"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A Gartner's duct cyst arises from a persistent caudal remnant of the mesonephric (Wolffian) duct and typically lies in the anterolateral wall of the upper vagina. Other mesonephric remnants in the female are the epoophoron and paroophoron in the broad ligament. Most are asymptomatic and need no treatment.","why_wrong":{"2":"Paramesonephric remnants include the hydatid of Morgagni and Müllerian cysts, not the classic anterolateral Gartner's cyst.","3":"A Bartholin cyst lies posterolaterally at the vaginal introitus (4 and 8 o'clock), not in the upper vaginal wall.","1":"Skene gland cysts lie beside the distal urethral meatus, not in the upper anterolateral vaginal wall.","4":"The gubernaculum forms the ovarian ligament and round ligament of the uterus, not vaginal cysts."}},{"id":8,"subject":"Anatomy of Reproductive and Genital Tract","topic":"Histology","domain":"knowledge","question":"In a 22-year-old woman the cervical transformation zone is visualised at colposcopy. Which physiological process normally occurs in this zone after puberty and in pregnancy, making it the site where cervical intraepithelial neoplasia develops?","options":["Atrophy of the endocervical stroma","Intestinal metaplasia of squamous epithelium","Squamous metaplasia of everted columnar epithelium","Keratinisation of the ectocervical squamous epithelium","Transitional (urothelial) metaplasia of glands"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Under oestrogen influence (puberty, pregnancy, combined pill) the endocervical columnar epithelium everts onto the ectocervix; exposed to acidic vaginal pH, its subcolumnar reserve cells undergo squamous metaplasia to form the transformation zone between the original and new squamocolumnar junctions. These actively dividing metaplastic cells are vulnerable to HPV, so CIN and squamous carcinoma arise here.","why_wrong":{"1":"Intestinal metaplasia is characteristic of the oesophagus (Barrett's) and stomach, not the cervix.","3":"The normal ectocervix is non-keratinised; keratinisation occurs only with prolapse or chronic exposure.","4":"Transitional metaplasia is not the physiological change of the transformation zone.","0":"Stromal atrophy is a postmenopausal change and is not what creates the transformation zone."}},{"id":9,"subject":"Anatomy of Reproductive and Genital Tract","topic":"Histology","domain":"knowledge","question":"An endometrial biopsy taken for infertility work-up shows glands with prominent subnuclear glycogen vacuoles that push the nuclei towards the lumen, with no stromal oedema or predecidual change. This appearance most accurately indicates which stage of the cycle?","options":["Menstrual phase","Late secretory phase","Early proliferative phase","Early secretory phase","Late proliferative phase"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Subnuclear vacuolation of the glandular epithelium appears about 36–48 hours after ovulation (cycle day 16–17) under the influence of progesterone and is the earliest histological evidence that ovulation has occurred. The vacuoles then move to a supranuclear position and secretion into the lumen follows, with stromal oedema and predecidual change in the mid-to-late secretory phase.","why_wrong":{"2":"Early proliferative endometrium has thin straight tubular glands with mitoses and no vacuoles.","4":"Late proliferative glands are tortuous with pseudostratified nuclei but lack glycogen vacuoles because there is no progesterone yet.","1":"Late secretory endometrium shows saw-tooth glands with luminal secretion and predecidual stromal change.","0":"Menstrual endometrium shows stromal breakdown, haemorrhage and fragmented glands."}},{"id":10,"subject":"Anatomy of Reproductive and Genital Tract","topic":"Developmental Defects of Genitourinary Tract","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 17-year-old girl presents with primary amenorrhoea. She has normal breast development and normal pubic and axillary hair. Examination shows a short blind-ending vagina with no cyclical pain. Ultrasound shows normal ovaries but no uterus. Serum testosterone is in the female range and karyotype is 46,XX. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Kallmann syndrome","Transverse vaginal septum","Mayer–Rokitansky–Küster–Hauser syndrome","Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome","Turner syndrome"],"answer":2,"explanation":"MRKH syndrome is congenital agenesis of the uterus and upper vagina due to failure of paramesonephric duct development in a 46,XX female with normal ovaries, so secondary sexual characteristics and hormone levels are normal. About a third have associated renal anomalies (e.g. unilateral renal agenesis) and some have skeletal anomalies, so renal ultrasound is part of the work-up. Treatment is vaginal dilator therapy first-line.","why_wrong":{"3":"CAIS has a 46,XY karyotype, male-range testosterone and scanty or absent pubic and axillary hair.","1":"A transverse septum leaves the uterus present, so there is cyclical pain and haematocolpos above the septum.","4":"Turner syndrome (45,X) causes streak ovaries with absent breast development and a normal uterus.","0":"Kallmann syndrome causes hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism with absent puberty and anosmia, but the uterus is present."}},{"id":11,"subject":"Anatomy of Reproductive and Genital Tract","topic":"Developmental Defects of Genitourinary Tract","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 14-year-old girl presents with monthly lower abdominal pain for 6 months, primary amenorrhoea and acute urinary retention. Secondary sexual characteristics are normal. A tense, bulging, bluish membrane is seen at the introitus, and a pelvic mass is palpable. What is the most appropriate definitive management?","options":["Diagnostic laparoscopy to exclude endometriosis","Cruciate (or elliptical) incision of the hymen with drainage","Needle aspiration of the haematocolpos through the membrane","Continuous combined oral contraceptive pill to suppress menses","Vaginoplasty using a split-thickness skin graft"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The picture is imperforate hymen with haematocolpos (bulging bluish membrane at the introitus; the distended vagina can cause urinary retention). Definitive treatment is a cruciate or elliptical incision of the hymen under anaesthesia, allowing the retained menstrual blood to drain, with excision of excess hymenal tissue to prevent re-closure.","why_wrong":{"2":"Needle aspiration alone does not relieve the obstruction, tends to re-accumulate and risks introducing infection (pyocolpos).","3":"Menstrual suppression may relieve pain temporarily but does not treat the outflow obstruction.","0":"Laparoscopy is not needed; the diagnosis is clinical and the obstruction is at the introitus.","4":"Skin-graft vaginoplasty is for vaginal agenesis, whereas here the vagina is present and only the hymen is imperforate."}},{"id":12,"subject":"Anatomy of Reproductive and Genital Tract","topic":"Developmental Defects of Genitourinary Tract","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 30-year-old woman has had three consecutive first-trimester miscarriages. Three-dimensional ultrasound shows a normal convex external fundal contour with a thick midline septum dividing the uterine cavity down to the internal os. Which embryological error explains this anomaly?","options":["Complete failure of fusion of the paramesonephric ducts","Failure of development of one paramesonephric duct","Incomplete resorption of the midline septum after paramesonephric duct fusion","Incomplete fusion of the paramesonephric ducts at the fundus","Agenesis of both paramesonephric ducts"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The paramesonephric ducts first fuse in the midline and the intervening septum is then resorbed. Failure of resorption gives a septate uterus, which has a normal external fundal contour (distinguishing it from a bicornuate uterus) and is the uterine anomaly most associated with recurrent miscarriage. 3D ultrasound or MRI is used to distinguish the external fundal contour.","why_wrong":{"3":"Incomplete fusion at the fundus gives a bicornuate uterus with an indented external fundal contour.","0":"Complete failure of fusion gives uterus didelphys with two cervices.","1":"Failure of one duct to develop gives a unicornuate uterus, often with a rudimentary horn and renal anomalies.","4":"Agenesis of both ducts causes MRKH syndrome with absent uterus and upper vagina."}},{"id":13,"subject":"Anatomy of Reproductive and Genital Tract","topic":"Breast","domain":"knowledge","question":"During a postnatal check, a 24-year-old woman notices a small pigmented nodule in her left axilla that became swollen and slightly tender when her milk came in. Examination shows a small accessory nipple with underlying breast tissue. This most likely results from which developmental abnormality?","options":["Persistence of part of the mammary ridge (milk line)","Persistence of the second pharyngeal cleft","Ectopic endodermal rest of thyroid tissue","Failure of canalisation of the lactiferous ducts","Hamartoma of the sweat glands of the axilla"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The breast develops from a band of thickened ectoderm, the mammary ridge (milk line), running from the axilla to the inguinal region. Normally only the pectoral portion persists; persistence of other parts gives polythelia (accessory nipple) or polymastia (accessory breast tissue), most commonly in the axilla, which can enlarge during pregnancy and lactation.","why_wrong":{"1":"A persistent second pharyngeal cleft gives a lateral cervical (branchial) cyst or sinus in the neck.","3":"Failure of duct canalisation would affect the normal breast, not produce an axillary accessory nipple.","2":"Ectopic thyroid lies along the thyroglossal tract in the midline of the neck.","4":"A sweat gland hamartoma would not contain a nipple or breast tissue that responds to lactation."}},{"id":14,"subject":"Anatomy of Reproductive and Genital Tract","topic":"Placenta","domain":"knowledge","question":"The definitive placenta consists of a fetal part and a maternal part. Which structure forms the maternal component of the placenta?","options":["Chorion laeve","Decidua capsularis","Decidua basalis","Decidua parietalis","Amnion"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The fetal component of the placenta is the chorion frondosum (villous chorion) and the maternal component is the decidua basalis, the decidua lying deep to the implantation site. In placenta accreta spectrum the decidua basalis (including the Nitabuch layer) is deficient, allowing villi to attach to or invade the myometrium.","why_wrong":{"1":"The decidua capsularis covers the conceptus on the luminal side and later fuses with the decidua parietalis.","3":"The decidua parietalis lines the rest of the uterine cavity and does not form the placenta.","0":"The chorion laeve is the smooth, fetal chorion that forms part of the membranes.","4":"The amnion is a fetal membrane lining the amniotic cavity, not the maternal part of the placenta."}},{"id":15,"subject":"Anatomy of Reproductive and Genital Tract","topic":"Placenta","domain":"knowledge","question":"In normal placentation, spiral arteries are converted into wide, low-resistance vessels by invading trophoblast. Failure of this remodelling is central to the pathogenesis of pre-eclampsia and fetal growth restriction. Which cell type is primarily responsible for the remodelling?","options":["Villous cytotrophoblast (Langhans cells)","Amniotic epithelial cells","Hofbauer cells","Extravillous (endovascular) cytotrophoblast","Syncytiotrophoblast"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Extravillous cytotrophoblast migrates out from the anchoring villi into the decidua and myometrium and, as endovascular trophoblast, replaces the endothelium and muscular wall of the spiral arteries. This turns them into dilated, low-resistance channels. In pre-eclampsia this invasion is shallow (limited to the decidual segments), leaving high-resistance vessels and placental ischaemia.","why_wrong":{"4":"Syncytiotrophoblast covers the villi and handles exchange and hormone production; it does not invade the arterial walls.","0":"Villous cytotrophoblast (Langhans layer) is the progenitor layer beneath the syncytium within the villi, not the invading cell.","2":"Hofbauer cells are fetal macrophages in the villous stroma.","1":"Amniotic epithelium lines the amniotic cavity and has no role in spiral artery remodelling."}},{"id":16,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Endocrinology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 35-year-old woman with bitemporal hemianopia has a 3 cm pituitary macroadenoma. Serum prolactin is mildly raised at 1,200 mIU/L (about 57 ng/mL). After surgery, immunohistochemistry confirms a non-functioning (gonadotroph) adenoma. What is the best explanation for her raised prolactin?","options":["Reduced renal clearance of prolactin","Prolactin secretion by the tumour cells","Excess oestrogen stimulating lactotroph hyperplasia","Increased hypothalamic TRH secretion","Interruption of dopamine delivery to lactotrophs by stalk compression"],"answer":4,"explanation":"Prolactin is the only anterior pituitary hormone under predominantly inhibitory hypothalamic control, by dopamine carried in the hypophyseal portal vessels. A large non-functioning adenoma compressing the stalk interrupts this dopamine supply ('stalk effect'), causing modest hyperprolactinaemia, usually below about 2,000 mIU/L, whereas a macroprolactinoma typically produces levels in the many thousands.","why_wrong":{"3":"Raised TRH causes hyperprolactinaemia in primary hypothyroidism, not with a non-functioning adenoma.","1":"The tumour was shown to be non-functioning, and a 3 cm prolactinoma would give a far higher prolactin level.","0":"Reduced renal clearance raises prolactin in chronic kidney disease, not in this setting.","2":"Oestrogen excess (e.g. pregnancy) stimulates lactotrophs but is not the mechanism here."}},{"id":17,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Endocrinology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 28-year-old woman had a massive postpartum haemorrhage with prolonged hypotension needing six units of blood. One week later her breasts have not become engorged and she produces no milk despite frequent suckling. Deficiency of which hormone most directly explains this?","options":["Growth hormone","Prolactin","Oxytocin","Thyroid-stimulating hormone","Adrenocorticotrophic hormone"],"answer":1,"explanation":"This is Sheehan's syndrome: ischaemic necrosis of the anterior pituitary, which enlarges in pregnancy and becomes vulnerable to hypotension. Failure of lactation from prolactin deficiency is usually the earliest feature, followed by amenorrhoea, hypothyroidism and adrenal insufficiency. Prolactin drives milk synthesis (galactopoiesis), whereas oxytocin from the posterior pituitary drives milk ejection by contracting myoepithelial cells.","why_wrong":{"2":"Oxytocin causes milk ejection, not milk production, and the posterior pituitary is usually spared in Sheehan's syndrome.","3":"TSH deficiency causes secondary hypothyroidism, which develops later and is not the direct cause of absent milk.","4":"ACTH deficiency causes secondary adrenal insufficiency but not failure of milk production.","0":"Growth hormone deficiency in adults causes fatigue and altered body composition, not lactation failure."}},{"id":18,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Endocrinology","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 32-year-old woman with severe endometriosis is started on a monthly depot GnRH agonist. She develops hot flushes and amenorrhoea after a brief initial worsening of symptoms. What is the mechanism by which continuous GnRH agonist therapy suppresses ovarian function?","options":["Competitive blockade of pituitary GnRH receptors from the first dose","Blockade of oestrogen receptors in the endometrium","Stimulation of hypothalamic dopamine release","Desensitisation and down-regulation of pituitary GnRH receptors after an initial flare","Direct inhibition of ovarian aromatase"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Physiological GnRH is released in pulses; pulsatility is essential for gonadotrophin secretion. A continuously present GnRH agonist first causes a flare of FSH and LH release, then desensitisation and down-regulation of pituitary GnRH receptors, producing a hypogonadotrophic, hypo-oestrogenic state (a 'medical oophorectomy'). Add-back HRT limits bone loss and vasomotor symptoms.","why_wrong":{"0":"Immediate competitive blockade without a flare is the mechanism of GnRH antagonists such as cetrorelix.","4":"Direct aromatase inhibition is the action of letrozole and anastrozole.","1":"Oestrogen receptor blockade describes SERMs or antioestrogens, not GnRH agonists.","2":"Dopamine inhibits prolactin; GnRH agonists do not act by dopamine release."}},{"id":19,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Endocrinology","domain":"knowledge","question":"According to the two-cell, two-gonadotrophin theory of ovarian steroidogenesis, theca cells produce androgens that diffuse into granulosa cells to be converted into oestradiol. Which hormone induces the aromatase enzyme in the granulosa cells?","options":["Anti-Müllerian hormone","Prolactin","Inhibin B","Follicle-stimulating hormone","Luteinising hormone"],"answer":3,"explanation":"LH acts on theca cells (which have LH receptors and cholesterol side-chain cleavage and 17α-hydroxylase activity) to produce androstenedione and testosterone. These diffuse to granulosa cells, where FSH induces aromatase to convert them to oestrone and oestradiol. Granulosa cells lack 17α-hydroxylase, so they depend on theca androgens.","why_wrong":{"4":"LH stimulates androgen production by theca cells; it does not induce granulosa aromatase in the follicular phase.","2":"Inhibin B is secreted by granulosa cells and selectively suppresses pituitary FSH.","0":"AMH from small follicles actually inhibits FSH-induced aromatase and follicle recruitment.","1":"Prolactin does not induce aromatase; high levels suppress GnRH and ovulation."}},{"id":20,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Menstrual Cycle","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 29-year-old woman with 2 years of subfertility has regular cycles every 35 days. To confirm ovulation with a mid-luteal serum progesterone, on which cycle day should the blood sample be taken?","options":["Day 2","Day 21","Day 35","Day 28","Day 14"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Serum progesterone should be measured in the mid-luteal phase, about 7 days before the expected next period. The luteal phase is relatively constant at about 14 days, so in a 35-day cycle ovulation occurs around day 21 and the sample is taken on day 28. A level above about 30 nmol/L indicates ovulation. 'Day 21 progesterone' applies only to a 28-day cycle.","why_wrong":{"0":"Day 2 is the early follicular phase, used for FSH, LH and oestradiol, not to confirm ovulation.","4":"Day 14 is before ovulation in a 35-day cycle, so progesterone would still be low.","1":"Day 21 is around the time of ovulation in a 35-day cycle, so the luteal peak has not yet occurred.","2":"Day 35 is when menses are due, by which time progesterone is falling with corpus luteum regression."}},{"id":21,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Menstrual Cycle","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 32-year-old woman with polycystic ovary syndrome has only three or four periods a year. She is not trying to conceive. Which long-term complication is a direct result of her chronic anovulation and unopposed oestrogen exposure?","options":["Endometrial hyperplasia","Ovarian germ cell tumour","Osteoporosis","Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia","Intrauterine adhesions"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In chronic anovulation there is no corpus luteum, so the endometrium is exposed to oestrogen without progesterone opposition, which leads to endometrial hyperplasia and an increased risk of endometrial carcinoma. Women with PCOS and fewer than about four periods a year are advised to have a progestogen-induced withdrawal bleed at least every 3–4 months (or use a COC or LNG-IUS), and to have a TVS if amenorrhoea is prolonged.","why_wrong":{"3":"CIN is caused by persistent high-risk HPV infection, not by anovulation.","2":"Oestrogen levels are maintained in PCOS, so osteoporosis is not a consequence of its anovulation.","1":"Germ cell tumours are not linked to PCOS or unopposed oestrogen.","4":"Intrauterine adhesions (Asherman's) follow endometrial trauma or infection, not anovulation."}},{"id":22,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Menstrual Cycle","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 28-year-old woman has had no periods for 8 months since a vigorous uterine curettage for secondary postpartum haemorrhage due to retained products. FSH, LH, TSH and prolactin are normal. She has no withdrawal bleed after a progestogen challenge or after a course of combined oestrogen and progestogen. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Premature ovarian insufficiency","Polycystic ovary syndrome","Hyperprolactinaemic amenorrhoea","Asherman syndrome","Sheehan syndrome"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Failure to bleed even after priming with oestrogen and progestogen localises the problem to the end organ or outflow tract. After vigorous curettage of the postpartum (or post-abortion) uterus, damage to the basal endometrium causes intrauterine adhesions: Asherman syndrome. It is confirmed and treated by hysteroscopy with adhesiolysis.","why_wrong":{"4":"Sheehan syndrome causes low gonadotrophins and failure of lactation, but the endometrium would still bleed after oestrogen–progestogen.","0":"POI would give raised FSH, and the endometrium would respond to exogenous hormones.","1":"PCOS usually produces a withdrawal bleed after progestogen because oestrogen levels are adequate.","2":"Prolactin is normal, and hyperprolactinaemic amenorrhoea would respond to combined hormone challenge."}},{"id":23,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Menstrual Cycle","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 14-year-old girl has had very heavy periods since menarche a year ago, with flooding and clots, and now has a haemoglobin of 7 g/dL. She also gives a history of frequent nosebleeds and prolonged bleeding after a dental extraction. Pelvic examination is not performed. Which investigation is most appropriate to identify the likely cause?","options":["Von Willebrand factor antigen and activity with coagulation screen","Diagnostic hysteroscopy","Transabdominal pelvic ultrasound","Endometrial biopsy","Serum FSH and LH"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In the PALM-COEIN classification of abnormal uterine bleeding, 'C' is coagulopathy. Heavy menstrual bleeding since menarche, especially with other bleeding symptoms, should prompt testing for a bleeding disorder, of which von Willebrand disease is the commonest (NICE). Testing includes vWF antigen, vWF activity, factor VIII and a coagulation screen, preferably with haematology input.","why_wrong":{"2":"Structural causes (polyps, fibroids, adenomyosis) are rare at this age, and imaging would not explain her other bleeding symptoms.","3":"Endometrial biopsy is for women at risk of hyperplasia or malignancy, not an adolescent with a likely bleeding disorder.","4":"Gonadotrophins help with anovulation or amenorrhoea but do not identify a coagulopathy.","1":"Hysteroscopy is for suspected structural intracavity pathology and is not first-line in an adolescent."}},{"id":24,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Menstrual Cycle","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 58-year-old woman who reached menopause at 51 and has never used HRT presents with a single episode of postmenopausal bleeding. Speculum examination is normal. Transvaginal ultrasound shows an endometrial thickness of 9 mm. What is the most appropriate next step?","options":["Start a cyclical progestogen","Repeat transvaginal ultrasound in 3 months","Reassure and discharge","Endometrial biopsy","Total hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Postmenopausal bleeding must be considered endometrial cancer until proven otherwise. An endometrial thickness above 4 mm on TVS in a woman not on HRT requires histological sampling, by outpatient Pipelle biopsy or hysteroscopy with directed biopsy. A thickness of 4 mm or less has a very low risk of carcinoma, although recurrent bleeding still needs investigation.","why_wrong":{"2":"Reassurance is unsafe because an endometrium over 4 mm needs histological assessment.","1":"Delaying with repeat imaging postpones diagnosis of a possible endometrial cancer.","0":"Progestogens without a tissue diagnosis may mask or delay the diagnosis of carcinoma.","4":"Major surgery should not be done without first obtaining a histological diagnosis."}},{"id":25,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Menstrual Cycle","domain":"knowledge","question":"The husband of a woman with 3 years of primary subfertility has a semen analysis: volume 3 mL, sperm concentration 40 million/mL, progressive motility 45% and normal forms 2% (strict criteria). How is this result best described?","options":["Teratozoospermia","Asthenozoospermia","Oligozoospermia","Normozoospermia","Azoospermia"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Volume, concentration and motility are within WHO reference limits, but normal morphology is below the lower reference limit of 4% (strict criteria, unchanged between the 2010 and 2021 WHO manuals), which is teratozoospermia. An abnormal result should be repeated, ideally after about 3 months (one spermatogenic cycle), before drawing conclusions (NICE).","why_wrong":{"2":"Oligozoospermia means low sperm concentration, but 40 million/mL is normal.","1":"Asthenozoospermia means reduced motility, but 45% progressive motility is normal.","4":"Azoospermia means no sperm in the ejaculate.","3":"Morphology of 2% normal forms is below the reference limit, so the sample is not normal."}},{"id":26,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Menstrual Cycle","domain":"attitude","question":"A 24-year-old unmarried woman attends the gynaecology OPD with intermenstrual bleeding for 3 months. A male house officer needs to perform a speculum examination to look at her cervix. She appears anxious and hesitant. What is the most appropriate approach?","options":["Proceed quickly with the speculum examination since it is essential for diagnosis and delay may harm her","Examine her without a chaperone so that her privacy and confidentiality are maintained","Obtain consent from her mother waiting outside, since she is unmarried","Avoid any pelvic assessment and advise her to return after marriage","Explain the purpose and nature of the examination, seek her consent, offer a female chaperone, and respect her decision if she declines"],"answer":4,"explanation":"An intimate examination requires a clear explanation of why it is needed and what it involves, valid consent from the patient herself (a competent adult), and the offer of a chaperone, ideally female, whatever the examiner's gender. Her dignity must be protected and she may withdraw consent at any time. If she declines a speculum, alternatives such as transabdominal ultrasound can be discussed and the decision documented.","why_wrong":{"0":"Examining an anxious patient without explanation and consent is unethical and may amount to assault.","2":"A competent adult gives her own consent; marital status does not transfer this right to her mother.","1":"Chaperones protect both the patient and the doctor and do not breach confidentiality; she should be offered one.","3":"Refusing to assess her could miss significant pathology (e.g. cervical lesions) and disregards her autonomy."}},{"id":27,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Disorders of Gonadal Hormone Production","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 2-week-old infant with a 46,XX karyotype was noted at birth to have clitoromegaly and partially fused labioscrotal folds. She now presents with vomiting, poor feeding and dehydration. Serum sodium is 124 mmol/L, potassium 6.8 mmol/L and 17-hydroxyprogesterone is markedly raised. Which enzyme is deficient?","options":["11β-hydroxylase","21-hydroxylase","17α-hydroxylase","5α-reductase","Aromatase"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia due to 21-hydroxylase deficiency is the commonest cause of ambiguous genitalia in a 46,XX infant. Blocked cortisol and aldosterone synthesis causes ACTH-driven accumulation of 17-hydroxyprogesterone, which is shunted to adrenal androgens and virilises the female fetus. Aldosterone deficiency gives a salt-wasting crisis with hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia at 1–3 weeks of life.","why_wrong":{"0":"11β-hydroxylase deficiency causes virilisation but with hypertension and hypokalaemia from deoxycorticosterone excess, not salt wasting.","2":"17α-hydroxylase deficiency blocks sex steroid synthesis, so a 46,XX infant is not virilised, and it causes hypertension.","3":"5α-reductase deficiency affects 46,XY individuals, causing undervirilised genitalia, with normal adrenal function.","4":"Aromatase deficiency can virilise a 46,XX fetus (and the mother) but does not cause salt wasting or raised 17-hydroxyprogesterone."}},{"id":28,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Disorders of Gonadal Hormone Production","domain":"attitude","question":"A 16-year-old girl raised as female is found to have complete androgen insensitivity syndrome (46,XY karyotype with intra-abdominal testes). Her parents ask the gynaecologist never to tell her about the karyotype or the testes, fearing it will destroy her identity. What is the most appropriate response?","options":["Postpone all discussion until after gonadectomy, which is performed without explaining its purpose","Leave the decision about whether she is ever told entirely to her parents","Agree to their request and tell her only that she has 'weak ovaries' that need removal","Acknowledge their fears, explain her right to know, and plan staged, age-appropriate disclosure with the parents and psychological support","Tell her the full diagnosis immediately at the next visit, without involving the parents"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Current consensus on disorders of sex development supports honest, planned disclosure: the young person has a right to information about her own body, and deception damages trust and later psychological health. The parents' concerns should be explored with empathy and disclosure planned in stages, at a pace suited to her maturity, by an experienced multidisciplinary team including a psychologist, ideally with parental involvement. She should understand and consent to any gonadectomy.","why_wrong":{"2":"Deliberately misleading her is dishonest, undermines trust and fails to obtain valid consent for surgery.","4":"Abrupt disclosure that ignores the family's concerns and psychological preparation can cause avoidable harm.","1":"At 16 she has her own right to information and to take part in decisions about her care.","0":"Operating without explaining its purpose invalidates consent and breaches her autonomy."}},{"id":29,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Menopause","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 34-year-old woman has had no periods for 6 months and complains of hot flushes and night sweats. A pregnancy test is negative. According to NICE guidance, which investigation is used to confirm a diagnosis of premature ovarian insufficiency?","options":["Serum FSH on two occasions 4–6 weeks apart","Transvaginal ultrasound for antral follicle count","Progestogen challenge test","A single serum oestradiol level","Single serum anti-Müllerian hormone"],"answer":0,"explanation":"NICE (NG23) diagnoses premature ovarian insufficiency in women under 40 with menopausal symptoms, including no or infrequent periods, plus an elevated FSH on two blood samples taken 4–6 weeks apart. Once diagnosed, causes (e.g. karyotype for Turner mosaicism, FMR1 premutation, autoimmune screen) are considered, and HRT or a combined pill is advised at least until the average age of natural menopause.","why_wrong":{"4":"NICE advises against using AMH alone to diagnose POI.","1":"Antral follicle count reflects ovarian reserve but is not the diagnostic test for POI.","2":"A progestogen challenge assesses oestrogen status and the outflow tract, not ovarian failure specifically.","3":"Oestradiol fluctuates widely and a single level cannot confirm the diagnosis."}},{"id":30,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Menopause","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 51-year-old woman with an intact uterus has troublesome vasomotor symptoms that are affecting her work. Her BMI is 36 kg/m² and she has varicose veins, but no personal history of thromboembolism or breast cancer. She wishes to start HRT. Which regimen is most appropriate?","options":["Vaginal oestrogen cream alone","Oral oestradiol alone","Transdermal oestradiol alone","Transdermal oestradiol with a progestogen","Oral oestradiol with a progestogen"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Women with a uterus need a progestogen added to oestrogen to prevent endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma. Oral oestrogen undergoes hepatic first-pass metabolism and increases clotting factor production and VTE risk, whereas transdermal oestradiol at standard doses does not appear to increase VTE risk above baseline. NICE therefore recommends transdermal rather than oral HRT for women at increased VTE risk, such as those with a BMI over 30.","why_wrong":{"1":"Unopposed oestrogen in a woman with a uterus risks endometrial hyperplasia, and the oral route adds VTE risk.","4":"Oral oestrogen increases VTE risk, which matters in an obese woman, making transdermal preferable.","2":"Transdermal oestrogen without a progestogen leaves the endometrium unopposed.","0":"Vaginal oestrogen treats urogenital atrophy but does not control systemic vasomotor symptoms."}},{"id":31,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Menopause","domain":"attitude","question":"A 32-year-old nulliparous woman has just been diagnosed with premature ovarian insufficiency. She becomes tearful and asks whether she will ever be able to have a baby. What is the most appropriate response?","options":["Tell her pregnancy is now impossible and she should consider adoption","Reassure her that her ovaries will recover and she will conceive once HRT is started","Explain honestly that spontaneous pregnancy is unlikely but possible, discuss options such as donor egg IVF, and offer psychological support","Avoid discussing fertility until she has accepted the diagnosis and simply prescribe HRT","Advise IVF with her own eggs as soon as possible as the most effective option"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Breaking difficult news requires honesty, empathy and space for her questions. In POI, ovarian function can be intermittent and about 5–10% of women conceive spontaneously, so she should not be told pregnancy is impossible, nor falsely reassured. Oocyte donation offers the best chance of pregnancy, and she should be offered counselling and psychological support. HRT is for symptoms and bone health, not contraception or a fertility treatment.","why_wrong":{"0":"This is factually wrong because spontaneous conception occurs in a minority of women with POI, and it removes hope unnecessarily.","1":"False reassurance is dishonest; HRT does not restore ovarian function.","4":"IVF with her own eggs has very poor success in POI because of depleted ovarian reserve.","3":"Avoiding the question she has directly asked is poor communication and leaves her without information."}},{"id":32,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Maternal Physiological Changes in Pregnancy","domain":"knowledge","question":"A healthy 26-year-old primigravida at 30 weeks is examined in the antenatal clinic. Which cardiovascular finding is NOT explained by normal pregnancy physiology and warrants further evaluation?","options":["Third heart sound","Soft ejection systolic murmur at the left sternal edge","Mild bilateral ankle oedema","Diastolic murmur","Left axis deviation on ECG"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Pregnancy increases blood volume and cardiac output (by about 40%) and lowers systemic vascular resistance. A soft ejection systolic flow murmur, a third heart sound, mild dependent oedema and a slight left axis shift from upward displacement of the heart are all common normal findings. A diastolic murmur is never physiological and should prompt echocardiography to look for structural heart disease such as mitral stenosis.","why_wrong":{"1":"A soft ejection systolic flow murmur is heard in most pregnant women because of increased flow.","0":"A third heart sound is common in normal pregnancy because of rapid ventricular filling with increased volume.","2":"Mild dependent oedema occurs with raised venous pressure in the legs and reduced oncotic pressure.","4":"The heart is displaced upwards and to the left by the enlarging uterus, producing a left axis shift."}},{"id":33,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Maternal Physiological Changes in Pregnancy","domain":"knowledge","question":"An arterial blood gas from a healthy woman at 34 weeks of pregnancy breathing room air shows: pH 7.44, PaCO2 30 mmHg (4.0 kPa), HCO3⁻ 20 mmol/L, PaO2 104 mmHg. What is the best interpretation?","options":["Mixed metabolic and respiratory acidosis","Compensated respiratory alkalosis, a physiological effect of pregnancy","Metabolic alkalosis","Acute respiratory acidosis","Partially compensated metabolic acidosis"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Progesterone increases the sensitivity of the respiratory centre to CO2, so tidal volume and minute ventilation rise (by about 30–50%) with an unchanged respiratory rate. PaCO2 falls to about 30 mmHg, and the kidneys compensate by excreting bicarbonate (to about 18–22 mmol/L), giving a compensated respiratory alkalosis with a slightly alkaline pH. The low maternal PaCO2 creates a gradient that favours CO2 transfer from the fetus.","why_wrong":{"4":"In primary metabolic acidosis the pH would be low; here it is slightly alkaline with a primary low PaCO2.","3":"Respiratory acidosis requires a raised PaCO2 and low pH, the opposite of these values.","2":"Metabolic alkalosis would show a raised bicarbonate, not a reduced one.","0":"The pH is alkaline and PaCO2 is low, which excludes any acidosis."}},{"id":34,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Maternal Physiological Changes in Pregnancy","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 30-year-old woman at 32 weeks of pregnancy has routine bloods showing a serum creatinine of 1.0 mg/dL (88 µmol/L). She has no symptoms. What is the best interpretation of this result?","options":["Abnormal only if accompanied by glycosuria","Uninterpretable, because creatinine cannot be measured reliably in pregnancy","Normal, because values up to 1.2 mg/dL are expected in the third trimester","Abnormal, because GFR rises by about 50% in pregnancy and creatinine normally falls to about 0.5–0.6 mg/dL","Normal, because renal blood flow increases in pregnancy and raises creatinine"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Renal plasma flow and GFR rise by about 50% from early pregnancy, so serum creatinine and urea fall; the normal pregnant creatinine is about 0.4–0.8 mg/dL (35–70 µmol/L). A value that is 'normal' outside pregnancy, such as 1.0 mg/dL, therefore suggests renal impairment and needs evaluation, for example for pre-eclampsia or underlying renal disease.","why_wrong":{"4":"Increased renal blood flow raises GFR and therefore lowers, not raises, serum creatinine.","2":"Pregnancy creatinine values are lower than non-pregnant values, so 1.0–1.2 mg/dL is not expected.","0":"Glycosuria is common in normal pregnancy because of increased filtered glucose and is unrelated to interpreting creatinine.","1":"Creatinine is reliably measured in pregnancy; it just needs pregnancy-specific reference ranges."}},{"id":35,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Maternal Physiological Changes in Pregnancy","domain":"knowledge","question":"In the fetal circulation, which vessel carries blood with the highest oxygen saturation?","options":["Descending aorta","Umbilical vein","Ductus arteriosus","Umbilical artery","Pulmonary trunk"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Oxygenated blood returns from the placenta in the single umbilical vein (saturation about 80%). About half bypasses the liver through the ductus venosus to the IVC, then is preferentially directed through the foramen ovale to the left heart and to the brain and coronary circulation. Deoxygenated blood returns to the placenta through the two umbilical arteries.","why_wrong":{"3":"The umbilical arteries carry deoxygenated blood from the fetus back to the placenta.","2":"The ductus arteriosus carries mainly less-oxygenated SVC blood from the right ventricle to the descending aorta.","4":"The pulmonary trunk carries right ventricular blood, which is relatively poorly oxygenated.","0":"The descending aorta receives mixed blood, including the less-oxygenated flow from the ductus arteriosus."}},{"id":36,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Placental Hormones","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 24-year-old woman at 9 weeks of pregnancy has severe hyperemesis gravidarum. Serum TSH is 0.05 mIU/L and free T4 is mildly raised. She has no goitre, no eye signs and no history of thyroid disease, and thyroid antibodies are negative. What is the most likely mechanism of her thyroid results?","options":["Release of preformed hormone from thyroiditis","Autoantibodies stimulating the TSH receptor","Increased thyroxine-binding globulin due to oestrogen","Stimulation of the TSH receptor by hCG","Increased iodine uptake due to iodine deficiency"],"answer":3,"explanation":"hCG shares its alpha subunit with TSH, and its beta subunit has structural homology with TSH, so at high concentrations (peaking at 8–12 weeks, and higher in hyperemesis, multiple and molar pregnancy) it weakly stimulates the TSH receptor. This gives gestational transient thyrotoxicosis with suppressed TSH and mildly raised free T4, which resolves by about 18–20 weeks and usually needs no antithyroid drugs.","why_wrong":{"1":"TSH receptor antibodies cause Graves' disease, usually with goitre or eye signs, and antibodies here are negative.","2":"Raised TBG increases total T4 but leaves free T4 and TSH normal.","0":"Thyroiditis-related thyrotoxicosis is not associated with hyperemesis, and painless thyroiditis typically occurs postpartum in antibody-positive women.","4":"Iodine deficiency tends to cause goitre and hypothyroidism, not suppressed TSH with raised free T4."}},{"id":37,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Placental Hormones","domain":"knowledge","question":"Insulin resistance increases progressively in the second half of pregnancy, sparing glucose for the fetus and predisposing some women to gestational diabetes. Which placental hormone, secreted by the syncytiotrophoblast in amounts proportional to placental mass, is the principal contributor?","options":["Oestriol","Inhibin A","Human chorionic gonadotrophin","Human placental lactogen","Relaxin"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Human placental lactogen (human chorionic somatomammotrophin) rises in parallel with placental mass. It is a growth hormone/prolactin-like hormone that antagonises insulin and promotes lipolysis, raising maternal free fatty acids for maternal fuel and sparing glucose and amino acids for the fetus. It is a major cause of the diabetogenic state of late pregnancy, which reverses rapidly after the placenta is delivered.","why_wrong":{"2":"hCG peaks in the first trimester, maintains the corpus luteum and is not the main cause of late insulin resistance.","4":"Relaxin, mainly from the corpus luteum, softens connective tissue and has no major diabetogenic effect.","1":"Inhibin A is a marker used in Down syndrome screening and does not cause insulin resistance.","0":"Oestriol reflects fetoplacental unit function (fetal adrenal and liver plus placenta) and is not the main anti-insulin hormone."}},{"id":38,"subject":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Placental Hormones","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 30-year-old woman in labour at term has artificial rupture of membranes at 4 cm dilatation. Immediately afterwards there is a sudden gush of bright red vaginal bleeding, and the CTG shows fetal bradycardia, although the mother remains haemodynamically stable and pain-free. Ultrasound earlier in pregnancy had reported a velamentous cord insertion. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Uterine rupture","Cervical laceration","Vasa praevia","Placental abruption","Placenta praevia"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In vasa praevia, fetal vessels running unsupported in the membranes (usually from a velamentous cord insertion or a succenturiate lobe) cross the internal os. Rupture of the membranes tears these vessels, so the bleeding is fetal blood. Because the fetal blood volume is small, fetal bradycardia and death follow rapidly while the mother is unaffected. Immediate category 1 caesarean section is needed.","why_wrong":{"3":"Abruption usually causes painful bleeding with a tender, tense uterus and is not linked to membrane rupture or cord insertion.","4":"Placenta praevia would normally be known from imaging, and bleeding is maternal, so the mother is compromised before the fetus.","0":"Uterine rupture typically causes pain, maternal shock and loss of station, usually in a scarred uterus.","1":"A cervical laceration would not cause sudden fetal compromise immediately after amniotomy."}},{"id":39,"subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Metabolic Pathways","domain":"knowledge","question":"In a woman with poorly controlled gestational diabetes, the fetus becomes hyperglycaemic because maternal glucose freely reaches it, whereas maternal insulin does not. By what mechanism does glucose cross the placenta?","options":["Simple diffusion through the lipid bilayer","Sodium-dependent active transport via SGLT1","Insulin-dependent transport via GLUT4","Facilitated diffusion via GLUT1 transporters","Receptor-mediated endocytosis"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Glucose, the main fetal energy substrate, crosses the syncytiotrophoblast by carrier-mediated facilitated diffusion down its concentration gradient, mainly through GLUT1 on the microvillous and basal membranes; it needs no energy and is not insulin-dependent. Fetal glucose therefore tracks maternal glucose, so maternal hyperglycaemia causes fetal hyperglycaemia, fetal hyperinsulinaemia and macrosomia (Pedersen hypothesis). Maternal insulin is too large to cross the placenta.","why_wrong":{"0":"Glucose is polar and cannot cross lipid membranes efficiently by simple diffusion; this route suits O2, CO2 and lipid-soluble drugs.","1":"Sodium-coupled active glucose transport (SGLT) occurs in the small intestine and renal tubule, not the placenta; amino acids are the substrates actively transported across the placenta.","2":"GLUT4 is the insulin-responsive transporter of skeletal muscle and fat, not the placental transporter.","4":"Receptor-mediated endocytosis (via FcRn) is how maternal IgG crosses the placenta."}},{"id":40,"subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Metabolic Pathways","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 15-year-old raised as a girl presents with primary amenorrhoea and progressive virilisation at puberty (deepening voice, phallic enlargement, increased muscle mass) without breast development. Karyotype is 46,XY, there are testes in both inguinal canals, no uterus, and a short blind-ending vagina. Serum testosterone is in the normal male range with a raised testosterone-to-dihydrotestosterone ratio. Which enzyme is deficient?","options":["21-Hydroxylase","Aromatase (CYP19)","17α-Hydroxylase","3β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","5α-Reductase type 2"],"answer":4,"explanation":"5α-Reductase type 2 converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone (DHT), which is required in fetal life to masculinise the external genitalia, urethra and prostate. Testosterone itself develops the Wolffian ducts and AMH from Sertoli cells regresses the Müllerian ducts, so there is no uterus. At puberty the surge of testosterone (and the type 1 isoenzyme) causes virilisation without breast development. Inheritance is autosomal recessive and it is more common in consanguineous communities; the raised T:DHT ratio is diagnostic.","why_wrong":{"0":"21-Hydroxylase deficiency virilises 46,XX females; it does not cause undervirilised 46,XY genitalia.","1":"Aromatase deficiency impairs oestrogen synthesis and virilises 46,XX fetuses (and their mothers); it would not raise the T:DHT ratio.","2":"17α-Hydroxylase deficiency blocks sex-steroid synthesis, so testosterone would be low, with hypertension and hypokalaemia.","3":"3β-HSD deficiency causes undervirilisation with low testosterone and usually salt-wasting adrenal insufficiency in infancy."}},{"id":41,"subject":"Biochemistry","topic":"Metabolic Pathways","domain":"knowledge","question":"The placenta produces large amounts of oestriol in pregnancy but cannot make oestrogens from cholesterol or progesterone on its own; it depends on dehydroepiandrosterone sulphate supplied by the fetal adrenal gland. The absence of which enzyme in the placenta explains this dependence?","options":["Aromatase (CYP19)","Steroid sulphatase","3β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","17α-Hydroxylase/17,20-lyase (CYP17)","Cholesterol side-chain cleavage enzyme (CYP11A1)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The placenta lacks CYP17 (17α-hydroxylase/17,20-lyase), so it cannot convert C21 steroids (pregnenolone, progesterone) into C19 androgens, the substrates for aromatase. This is the basis of the fetoplacental unit: fetal adrenal DHEA-S is 16α-hydroxylated in the fetal liver, then desulphated and aromatised in the placenta to oestriol. Hence maternal oestriol is low with anencephaly (fetal adrenal hypoplasia), placental sulphatase deficiency and fetal death.","why_wrong":{"0":"The placenta is rich in aromatase; its deficiency causes virilisation of both mother and a female fetus.","1":"Placental sulphatase is normally present; its X-linked deficiency causes low oestriol but it is not the enzyme the placenta lacks.","2":"The placenta has 3β-HSD, which it uses to convert pregnenolone to progesterone.","4":"The placenta has CYP11A1 and converts maternal LDL cholesterol to pregnenolone, so it makes progesterone autonomously."}},{"id":42,"subject":"Maternal Pharmacology","topic":"Teratogenic Drugs","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 29-year-old woman with rheumatic heart disease and a mechanical mitral valve prosthesis continued her regular long-term medication throughout the first trimester. Her newborn has a flattened, hypoplastic nasal bridge and radiographs show stippled epiphyses. Which drug is most likely responsible?","options":["Phenytoin","Sodium valproate","Lithium","Warfarin","Isotretinoin"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Warfarin embryopathy follows exposure at about 6–12 weeks and comprises nasal hypoplasia and chondrodysplasia punctata (stippled epiphyses); later exposure can cause fetal intracranial haemorrhage and CNS defects. The risk is dose-related (higher above 5 mg/day). Low-molecular-weight heparin does not cross the placenta and is the usual first-trimester alternative, though it needs close anti-Xa monitoring in women with mechanical valves.","why_wrong":{"0":"Fetal hydantoin syndrome causes craniofacial dysmorphism, hypoplastic distal phalanges and nails, and growth restriction.","1":"Valproate is chiefly associated with neural tube defects and neurodevelopmental impairment.","2":"Lithium is associated with Ebstein anomaly of the tricuspid valve.","4":"Isotretinoin causes ear anomalies (microtia), CNS malformations, conotruncal heart defects and thymic hypoplasia."}},{"id":43,"subject":"Maternal Pharmacology","topic":"Teratogenic Drugs","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 22-year-old woman is starting oral isotretinoin for severe nodulocystic acne under a pregnancy prevention programme. She is counselled to use highly effective contraception before and during treatment and to have regular pregnancy tests. For how long after the last dose must effective contraception continue?","options":["1 month","3 months","6 months","2 years","3 years"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Isotretinoin is a potent teratogen (retinoic acid embryopathy: microtia, CNS, cardiac and thymic defects). Under the MHRA/EMA pregnancy prevention programme, highly effective contraception is required for at least 1 month before, throughout and for 1 month after stopping, with pregnancy testing. Contrast acitretin, where contraception must continue for 3 years after stopping.","why_wrong":{"1":"Isotretinoin has a short half-life and is eliminated well within a month, so a 3-month washout is not the requirement.","2":"Six months is not the isotretinoin requirement; such prolonged contraception is unnecessary for this short-acting retinoid.","3":"A washout of up to 2 years applies to leflunomide (if no accelerated elimination), not isotretinoin.","4":"Three years applies to acitretin, which can be converted to the long-lived etretinate, not to isotretinoin."}},{"id":44,"subject":"Maternal Pharmacology","topic":"Drugs in Breast Milk","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 30-year-old exclusively breastfeeding woman was discharged after caesarean section with an oral analgesic for wound pain. On day 6 her baby is brought in drowsy, feeding poorly and having episodes of apnoea. Sepsis screen and blood glucose are normal. Which analgesic is most likely responsible?","options":["Paracetamol","Ibuprofen","Codeine phosphate","Mefenamic acid","Diclofenac"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Codeine is a prodrug converted by CYP2D6 to morphine; in mothers who are ultra-rapid metabolisers, high morphine levels enter breast milk and cause neonatal drowsiness, poor feeding and respiratory depression (deaths have been reported). The MHRA therefore contraindicates codeine in breastfeeding mothers. Paracetamol and ibuprofen are the analgesics of choice.","why_wrong":{"0":"Only tiny amounts of paracetamol reach breast milk; it is a safe first-line analgesic in breastfeeding.","1":"Ibuprofen has minimal milk transfer and a short half-life, making it the preferred NSAID in breastfeeding.","3":"Mefenamic acid passes into milk in small amounts and does not cause neonatal sedation or apnoea.","4":"Diclofenac is considered compatible with breastfeeding and does not cause CNS depression."}},{"id":45,"subject":"Maternal Pharmacology","topic":"Anaesthetics","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 28-year-old woman receives spinal anaesthesia with hyperbaric bupivacaine for elective caesarean section in left lateral tilt. Five minutes later she feels nauseated and her blood pressure falls from 120/75 to 82/45 mmHg with a heart rate of 98/min. Which vasopressor is currently recommended as first choice?","options":["Phenylephrine","Adrenaline","Dopamine","Dobutamine","Vasopressin"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Hypotension is the commonest complication of spinal anaesthesia for caesarean section, caused by sympathetic blockade (arterial and venous dilatation) and worsened by aortocaval compression. The 2018 international consensus recommends phenylephrine, an α1-agonist, ideally as a prophylactic infusion, because it causes less fetal acidosis than ephedrine. Watch for reflex bradycardia with phenylephrine.","why_wrong":{"1":"Adrenaline is reserved for anaphylaxis and cardiac arrest, not routine spinal-induced hypotension.","2":"Dopamine is an inotrope infusion for shock and is not used to treat spinal hypotension at caesarean section.","3":"Dobutamine is a β1 inotrope with vasodilator effect, which would worsen vasodilatory hypotension.","4":"Vasopressin is reserved for refractory vasodilatory shock, not routine obstetric spinal hypotension."}},{"id":46,"subject":"Maternal Pharmacology","topic":"Anaesthetics","domain":"knowledge","question":"During a top-up of her labour epidural with 0.25% bupivacaine, a woman complains of circumoral tingling and ringing in her ears, then has a generalised seizure followed by a broad-complex ventricular arrhythmia. Besides airway support, seizure control and cardiopulmonary resuscitation, which specific treatment should be given?","options":["Naloxone","Dantrolene","20% lipid emulsion","Flumazenil","Sodium bicarbonate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"This is local anaesthetic systemic toxicity, usually from inadvertent intravascular injection; CNS features (perioral numbness, tinnitus, metallic taste, seizures) typically precede cardiovascular collapse, and bupivacaine is especially cardiotoxic. AAGBI guidance is IV 20% lipid emulsion, 1.5 mL/kg bolus followed by an infusion, which acts as a 'lipid sink'. Prolonged resuscitation may be needed, and test doses with slow incremental injection help prevent it.","why_wrong":{"0":"Naloxone reverses opioid toxicity, such as respiratory depression from neuraxial opioids, not local anaesthetic toxicity.","1":"Dantrolene treats malignant hyperthermia triggered by volatile agents or suxamethonium.","3":"Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines and can itself precipitate seizures.","4":"Sodium bicarbonate is used for tricyclic antidepressant toxicity and is not the specific therapy for local anaesthetic toxicity."}},{"id":47,"subject":"Maternal Pharmacology","topic":"Tocolytics","domain":"knowledge","question":"A woman at 33 weeks' gestation in threatened preterm labour receives a tocolytic for several days. Serial scans show reducing amniotic fluid volume, and fetal echocardiography shows narrowing of the ductus arteriosus with tricuspid regurgitation. Which tocolytic was most likely used?","options":["Nifedipine","Atosiban","Terbutaline","Indomethacin","Magnesium sulphate"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Indomethacin inhibits cyclo-oxygenase and so prostaglandin synthesis; fetal PGE2 keeps the ductus arteriosus patent, so its use (especially after 32 weeks) risks premature ductal constriction. It also reduces fetal renal blood flow, causing oligohydramnios, and has been linked to NEC and IVH. Hence NSAID tocolysis is generally limited to under 32 weeks and to 48–72 hours.","why_wrong":{"0":"Nifedipine causes maternal headache, flushing, hypotension and reflex tachycardia but has no effect on the ductus.","1":"Atosiban has few side effects (mainly nausea) and does not affect the ductus or amniotic fluid.","2":"Terbutaline causes maternal tachycardia, hyperglycaemia, hypokalaemia and pulmonary oedema, not ductal constriction.","4":"Magnesium causes flushing, loss of reflexes and respiratory depression; it does not constrict the ductus."}},{"id":48,"subject":"Maternal Pharmacology","topic":"Tocolytics","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 26-year-old woman with moderate mitral stenosis presents at 30 weeks in threatened preterm labour. Tocolysis is planned to allow completion of a course of antenatal corticosteroids. Which agent is preferred because it has the fewest maternal cardiovascular side effects?","options":["Ritodrine","Nifedipine","Salbutamol","Glyceryl trinitrate","Atosiban"],"answer":4,"explanation":"Atosiban is a competitive oxytocin (and vasopressin V1a) receptor antagonist that reduces IP3-mediated calcium release in the myometrium. It has very few maternal side effects, so it is preferred where cardiovascular stability matters, such as in maternal cardiac disease. Tachycardia from β-agonists or reflex tachycardia from nifedipine shortens diastolic filling time and can precipitate pulmonary oedema in mitral stenosis.","why_wrong":{"0":"Ritodrine is a β2-agonist that causes tachycardia and pulmonary oedema and is contraindicated in heart disease.","1":"Nifedipine causes vasodilatation, hypotension and reflex tachycardia, which are poorly tolerated in mitral stenosis.","2":"Salbutamol, a β2-agonist, causes tachycardia, arrhythmias and pulmonary oedema.","3":"Glyceryl trinitrate is a nitric oxide donor that causes hypotension and headache and is not a first-choice tocolytic."}},{"id":49,"subject":"Maternal Pharmacology","topic":"Drugs for Labour Induction","domain":"knowledge","question":"A primigravida undergoing induction of labour has been on a high-dose oxytocin infusion diluted in 5% dextrose for 18 hours. She becomes confused and has a generalised seizure. BP is 118/74 mmHg, urine protein is negative, capillary glucose is 6.1 mmol/L and serum sodium is 117 mmol/L. What is the most likely mechanism of her condition?","options":["Eclamptic cerebral vasospasm","Antidiuretic effect of oxytocin","Amniotic fluid embolism","Hypoglycaemia from rebound insulin release","Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Oxytocin differs from vasopressin by only two amino acids, and at high doses it acts on renal V2 receptors to cause free-water retention. Combined with large volumes of hypotonic dextrose this produces dilutional hyponatraemia (water intoxication), with confusion, seizures and coma. Prevention: use concentrated oxytocin in normal saline or Hartmann's, monitor fluid balance and avoid prolonged high-dose infusions.","why_wrong":{"0":"Eclampsia is unlikely with normal blood pressure, no proteinuria and profound hyponatraemia.","2":"Amniotic fluid embolism presents with sudden cardiorespiratory collapse, hypoxia and DIC, not isolated hyponatraemia.","3":"Her capillary glucose is normal, so hypoglycaemia does not explain the seizure.","4":"Venous sinus thrombosis causes headache and focal signs, usually postpartum, and does not cause severe hyponatraemia."}},{"id":50,"subject":"Maternal Pharmacology","topic":"Drugs for Labour Induction","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 32-year-old para 3 with long-standing asthma has an atonic postpartum haemorrhage after vaginal delivery. Uterine massage, oxytocin infusion and tranexamic acid have been given but bleeding continues. Her BP is 110/70 mmHg. Which uterotonic should be avoided in her?","options":["Carboprost","Ergometrine","Misoprostol","Carbetocin","Oxytocin"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Carboprost (15-methyl PGF2α, 250 µg IM every 15 minutes, up to 8 doses) is effective for atonic PPH, but PGF2α causes bronchial smooth muscle contraction and can provoke severe bronchospasm, so it should be avoided (or used with great caution) in asthma. Ergometrine, by contrast, is avoided in hypertension and pre-eclampsia because of vasoconstriction. In a normotensive asthmatic, ergometrine and misoprostol are suitable second-line uterotonics; carbetocin is used mainly for prevention rather than treatment of PPH.","why_wrong":{"1":"Ergometrine is contraindicated in hypertension, pre-eclampsia and cardiac disease, not asthma, and her BP is normal.","2":"Misoprostol is a PGE1 analogue that does not cause bronchospasm and is safe in asthma.","3":"Carbetocin is a long-acting oxytocin analogue with no bronchoconstrictor effect.","4":"Oxytocin is the first-line uterotonic and does not cause bronchospasm."}},{"id":51,"subject":"Maternal Pharmacology","topic":"Drugs for Labour Induction","domain":"knowledge","question":"A woman has a primary postpartum haemorrhage of 1200 mL after vaginal birth. According to WHO recommendations based on the WOMAN trial, intravenous tranexamic acid 1 g should be given as soon as possible after diagnosis, but only if it can be started within what time of birth?","options":["1 hour","3 hours","6 hours","12 hours","24 hours"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Tranexamic acid is a synthetic lysine analogue that blocks the lysine-binding sites on plasminogen, inhibiting fibrinolysis. The WOMAN trial (2017) showed fewer deaths from bleeding when it was given within 3 hours of birth, with no benefit after this. WHO recommends 1 g IV over 10 minutes within 3 hours, with a second 1 g dose if bleeding continues after 30 minutes or restarts within 24 hours.","why_wrong":{"0":"Earlier is better, but benefit has been shown up to 3 hours, so 1 hour is not the cut-off.","2":"No benefit was found when treatment started more than 3 hours after birth.","3":"Late administration beyond 3 hours showed no benefit and is not recommended.","4":"Twenty-four hours is the window for a repeat dose if bleeding restarts, not for starting treatment."}},{"id":52,"subject":"Maternal Pharmacology","topic":"Hormonal Treatments and Contraception","domain":"attitude","question":"A 34-year-old mentally competent para 5 requests tubal ligation at her planned elective caesarean section. At her antenatal visits she was counselled about permanence, failure rate and long-acting reversible alternatives, and she remains certain. Her husband is working abroad and cannot be contacted. What is the most appropriate course of action regarding consent?","options":["Postpone sterilisation until her husband's written consent is obtained","Obtain written consent from her mother-in-law as next of kin","Proceed on her own informed written consent, documenting the counselling","Refer the decision to the hospital ethics committee first","Tell her sterilisation is only allowed six weeks or more after delivery"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Only the competent woman's own informed consent is legally and ethically required for her sterilisation; involving her partner is encouraged with her permission, but he has no right of veto. Counselling should cover permanence, the lifetime failure rate (about 1 in 200), ectopic risk if it fails, alternatives such as LARC and vasectomy, and the higher regret rate when done at caesarean section. The decision should ideally be made well before the operation, and the counselling documented.","why_wrong":{"0":"Spousal consent is not a legal requirement, and making it a precondition overrides a competent woman's autonomy.","1":"No relative can give or withhold consent on behalf of a competent adult.","3":"An ethics committee is not needed for a routine procedure on a competent, consenting adult.","4":"Sterilisation can be done at caesarean section; the higher regret and failure rates simply need to be discussed."}},{"id":53,"subject":"Maternal Pharmacology","topic":"Hormonal Treatments and Contraception","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 27-year-old non-smoking woman with a BMI of 23 kg/m² requests contraception. Her headaches are preceded by 20 minutes of flashing zigzag lines in her vision. For which of the following methods is her condition UKMEC category 4 (unacceptable health risk)?","options":["Combined oral contraceptive pill","Levonorgestrel intrauterine system","Copper intrauterine device","Etonogestrel implant","Progestogen-only pill"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Migraine with aura at any age is UKMEC 4 for all combined hormonal contraception (pill, patch and ring) because oestrogen further increases the already raised risk of ischaemic stroke. Progestogen-only methods are UKMEC 2 and the copper IUD UKMEC 1. Typical aura is visual (zigzag lines, scotomas) lasting 5–60 minutes before the headache.","why_wrong":{"1":"Progestogen-only methods such as the LNG-IUS are UKMEC 2 in migraine with aura.","2":"The copper IUD is non-hormonal and is UKMEC 1 for migraine of any type.","3":"The implant is UKMEC 2 for migraine with aura because it contains no oestrogen.","4":"The progestogen-only pill is UKMEC 2 for migraine with aura."}},{"id":54,"subject":"Maternal Pharmacology","topic":"Hormonal Treatments and Contraception","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 24-year-old woman with regular 28-day cycles attends 96 hours after a single episode of unprotected intercourse on day 12 of her cycle. She has no contraindications to any method. Which method of emergency contraception is most effective for her?","options":["Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg orally","Ulipristal acetate 30 mg orally","Copper intrauterine device","Combined pill (Yuzpe regimen)","Progestogen-only pill started immediately"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The copper IUD is the most effective emergency contraceptive (failure under 0.1%); it is toxic to sperm and ova and prevents implantation, and it can be inserted up to 120 hours after the first unprotected intercourse or up to 5 days after the earliest expected ovulation. It also provides ongoing contraception. Oral methods work mainly by delaying ovulation and fail if it has already occurred, as is likely here (day 16 of a 28-day cycle).","why_wrong":{"0":"Levonorgestrel is licensed only up to 72 hours and is the least effective oral option, failing once the LH surge has begun.","1":"Ulipristal works up to 120 hours but, like all oral methods, cannot prevent pregnancy once ovulation has occurred.","3":"The Yuzpe regimen is less effective, causes more nausea and is now obsolete.","4":"The progestogen-only pill is not an emergency contraceptive and would not prevent this pregnancy."}},{"id":55,"subject":"Maternal Pharmacology","topic":"Hormonal Treatments and Contraception","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 38-year-old para 3 has had heavy but regular periods for a year that are affecting her work. Pelvic examination and ultrasound are normal and her Hb is 10.8 g/dL. She also wants reliable contraception. According to NICE guidance, what is the first-line treatment?","options":["Tranexamic acid during menses","Cyclical oral norethisterone (days 5–26)","Endometrial ablation","Mefenamic acid during menses","Levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system"],"answer":4,"explanation":"NICE (NG88) recommends the 52 mg LNG-IUS as first-line treatment for heavy menstrual bleeding with no identified pathology, fibroids smaller than 3 cm or adenomyosis. Local progestogen causes endometrial atrophy, cutting blood loss by about 90%, and it doubles as long-acting contraception. If declined, tranexamic acid, NSAIDs, combined hormonal contraception or cyclical progestogens are alternatives.","why_wrong":{"0":"Tranexamic acid is a second-line option if the LNG-IUS is declined, and it gives no contraception.","1":"Cyclical oral progestogens are a less effective second-line option per NICE.","2":"Ablation is not first line, is only for women who have completed their family, and still needs separate contraception.","3":"Mefenamic acid gives only a modest reduction in blood loss, is second line and is not contraceptive."}},{"id":56,"subject":"Maternal Pharmacology","topic":"Hormonal Treatments and Contraception","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 26-year-old para 1 asks for contraception now but plans to conceive again in about 12 months, so she wants her fertility to return promptly when she stops. Which method is most likely to delay the return of her fertility?","options":["Depot medroxyprogesterone acetate injection","Copper intrauterine device","Etonogestrel subdermal implant","Combined oral contraceptive pill","Levonorgestrel intrauterine system"],"answer":0,"explanation":"After the last DMPA (Depo-Provera) injection, return of ovulation is delayed — the median time to conception is about 9–10 months after the last injection, and it can take up to a year or more. DMPA is therefore not ideal for women planning pregnancy within the next year. It also causes weight gain and a small reversible reduction in bone mineral density. With all the other listed methods fertility returns promptly after stopping or removal.","why_wrong":{"1":"Fertility returns immediately after removal of a copper IUD.","2":"Ovulation returns within days to weeks of implant removal.","3":"Fertility returns rapidly after stopping the COCP, usually within 1–3 cycles.","4":"Fertility returns promptly after LNG-IUS removal because its effect is mainly local."}},{"id":57,"subject":"Maternal Pharmacology","topic":"Miscellaneous","domain":"knowledge","question":"A woman with severe pre-eclampsia is on magnesium sulphate (4 g IV loading dose, then 1 g/hour). Six hours later her patellar reflexes are absent, respiratory rate is 9/min and urine output has been 15 mL/hour for the last 4 hours. The infusion is stopped. Which drug should be given next?","options":["Naloxone","Furosemide","Diazepam","Sodium bicarbonate","Calcium gluconate"],"answer":4,"explanation":"Magnesium is excreted renally, so oliguria leads to accumulation. Loss of patellar reflexes is the earliest sign of toxicity, followed by respiratory depression and then cardiac arrest, which is why reflexes, respiratory rate and urine output are monitored. The antidote is 10 mL of 10% calcium gluconate IV over 10 minutes, which antagonises magnesium at the neuromuscular junction.","why_wrong":{"0":"Naloxone reverses opioid-induced respiratory depression, not magnesium toxicity.","1":"Furosemide is not the antidote and does not rapidly reverse neuromuscular or respiratory depression.","2":"Diazepam would worsen respiratory depression and is not indicated for magnesium toxicity.","3":"Sodium bicarbonate does not antagonise magnesium at the neuromuscular junction."}},{"id":58,"subject":"Maternal Pharmacology","topic":"Miscellaneous","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 29-year-old woman at 26 weeks has a 75 g OGTT with a fasting plasma glucose of 7.4 mmol/L and a 2-hour value of 10.2 mmol/L. Fetal growth on ultrasound is normal. According to NICE guidance, what is the most appropriate initial management?","options":["Diet and exercise advice with review in 1–2 weeks","Metformin alone","Glibenclamide","Insulin, with or without metformin","Repeat the OGTT at 32 weeks"],"answer":3,"explanation":"NICE (NG3) recommends immediate insulin, with or without metformin, for gestational diabetes when fasting plasma glucose at diagnosis is 7.0 mmol/L or above (and considered at 6.0–6.9 if there are complications such as macrosomia or polyhydramnios). Below this, diet and exercise are tried first, with metformin added if targets are not met in 1–2 weeks. Insulin does not cross the placenta; NICE diagnostic thresholds for GDM are fasting ≥5.6 or 2-hour ≥7.8 mmol/L.","why_wrong":{"0":"Diet and exercise alone are offered first only when fasting glucose at diagnosis is below 7.0 mmol/L.","1":"Metformin alone is used when diet fails and fasting glucose at diagnosis is below 7.0 mmol/L.","2":"Glibenclamide is considered only if metformin is unsuitable and insulin is declined.","4":"The diagnosis is already established and delaying treatment risks macrosomia and stillbirth."}},{"id":59,"subject":"Maternal Pharmacology","topic":"Miscellaneous","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 30-year-old primigravida at 34 weeks with severe asthma requiring frequent inhaler use presents with BP 164/110 mmHg and significant proteinuria. Which antihypertensive is the most appropriate first choice for her?","options":["Intravenous labetalol","Oral atenolol","Oral nifedipine","Oral enalapril","Oral losartan"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Severe hypertension in pregnancy (≥160/110 mmHg) must be treated promptly; NICE first-line options are labetalol, nifedipine or methyldopa. Labetalol is contraindicated in asthma, so the calcium-channel blocker nifedipine is the best choice here, with IV hydralazine an alternative in critical care. ACE inhibitors and ARBs are avoided in pregnancy because of fetal renal toxicity.","why_wrong":{"0":"Labetalol is a β-blocker and is contraindicated in asthma because it can precipitate bronchospasm.","1":"Atenolol is a β-blocker linked to fetal growth restriction and still carries a bronchospasm risk.","3":"ACE inhibitors are fetotoxic, causing fetal renal failure, oligohydramnios and skull ossification defects.","4":"Angiotensin receptor blockers have the same fetotoxicity as ACE inhibitors and are contraindicated in pregnancy."}},{"id":60,"subject":"Maternal Pharmacology","topic":"Miscellaneous","domain":"attitude","question":"A house officer gives intravenous co-amoxiclav to a postnatal woman with suspected endometritis, then notices that her drug chart clearly records a penicillin allergy. She develops a mild urticarial rash that settles with an antihistamine, and her observations stay stable. What is the most appropriate next step?","options":["Say nothing, as the rash has settled and she would only be upset","Tell her she had an unexpected reaction to a new antibiotic","Inform only the consultant and leave disclosure to a later date","Record the event in her notes without discussing it with her","Explain the error honestly, apologise and file an incident report"],"answer":4,"explanation":"The duty of candour requires doctors to be open and honest with patients when something goes wrong: explain what happened, apologise, describe the likely consequences and what will be done to put it right. The error should also be reported through the incident-reporting system so the system can learn and prevent recurrence, and the senior team informed. Honest disclosure maintains trust and is expected under the GMC and PMDC codes of ethics.","why_wrong":{"0":"Concealing an error breaches the professional duty of candour and erodes trust.","1":"This is a misleading half-truth; the reaction was foreseeable from her documented allergy.","2":"Senior staff should be told, but disclosure to the patient should not be withheld or delayed.","3":"Documentation without telling the patient is not open disclosure."}},{"id":61,"subject":"Prenatal and Antenatal Care","topic":"Antenatal Care Overview","domain":"knowledge","question":"A woman with regular 35-day menstrual cycles books in the first trimester. The first day of her last menstrual period was 10 March 2026. Using Naegele's rule corrected for her cycle length, what is her expected date of delivery?","options":["10 December 2026","17 December 2026","24 December 2026","31 December 2026","3 January 2027"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Naegele's rule: EDD = first day of LMP + 9 months + 7 days, assuming a 28-day cycle with ovulation on day 14. For a longer cycle, add the extra days (35 − 28 = 7), so 10 March → 17 December → 24 December. In practice NICE recommends dating by crown–rump length at 10+0 to 13+6 weeks, which supersedes the LMP date.","why_wrong":{"0":"This adds 9 months only and forgets the 7 days of Naegele's rule.","1":"This is the uncorrected Naegele date, which assumes a 28-day cycle.","3":"This adds 14 days instead of 7 for the cycle correction.","4":"This over-corrects well beyond the 7-day difference in cycle length."}},{"id":62,"subject":"Prenatal and Antenatal Care","topic":"Antenatal Care Overview","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 25-year-old primigravida's booking investigations show blood group O Rh(D) negative with a negative antibody screen. Her husband is Rh(D) positive and she has had no sensitising events. When should routine antenatal anti-D prophylaxis be given?","options":["At 28 weeks","At booking, around 12 weeks","At 20 weeks with the anomaly scan","At 36 weeks","Only within 72 hours after delivery"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Routine antenatal anti-D prophylaxis is given to non-sensitised Rh(D)-negative women either as a single dose at 28 weeks or as two doses at 28 and 34 weeks, after a repeat group and antibody screen at 28 weeks. It prevents sensitisation from silent fetomaternal haemorrhage in the third trimester. A further postnatal dose is given within 72 hours if the baby is Rh(D) positive, adjusted by Kleihauer test.","why_wrong":{"1":"Routine prophylaxis at booking is not recommended; early anti-D is given only after sensitising events.","2":"Twenty weeks is too early to cover the third-trimester period when silent fetomaternal haemorrhage is most likely.","3":"Starting at 36 weeks would leave her unprotected for much of the third trimester.","4":"The postnatal dose is additional; postnatal anti-D alone misses sensitisation from silent antenatal bleeds."}},{"id":63,"subject":"Prenatal and Antenatal Care","topic":"Labour and Puerperium","domain":"knowledge","question":"During normal labour, the fetus is in a well-flexed vertex presentation in the occipito-anterior position. Which anteroposterior diameter of the fetal head presents to the pelvis?","options":["Occipitofrontal, 11.5 cm","Suboccipitobregmatic, 9.5 cm","Mentovertical, 13 cm","Submentobregmatic, 9.5 cm","Suboccipitofrontal, 10 cm"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A well-flexed vertex presents the smallest anteroposterior diameter, the suboccipitobregmatic (9.5 cm), with the biparietal (9.5 cm) as the transverse diameter. Increasing deflexion presents progressively larger diameters: suboccipitofrontal, occipitofrontal (OP positions) and mentovertical (brow). In face presentation the diameter falls again to submentobregmatic (9.5 cm).","why_wrong":{"0":"The occipitofrontal diameter presents when the head is deflexed, typically in occipito-posterior positions.","2":"The mentovertical diameter presents in brow presentation, which is undeliverable vaginally at term.","3":"The submentobregmatic diameter presents in a fully extended face presentation.","4":"The suboccipitofrontal diameter presents with incomplete flexion of the vertex."}},{"id":64,"subject":"Prenatal and Antenatal Care","topic":"Labour and Puerperium","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 24-year-old primigravida delivers a healthy baby after an uncomplicated labour. As part of active management of the third stage of labour, which uterotonic regimen does WHO recommend as first choice?","options":["Ergometrine 0.5 mg IV","Misoprostol 600 µg orally","Carboprost 250 µg IM","Oxytocin 10 IU IM or IV","Oxytocin 40 IU IV bolus"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Active management of the third stage reduces PPH by about 60% and consists of a prophylactic uterotonic soon after birth, delayed cord clamping (1–3 minutes) and controlled cord traction by a skilled attendant. WHO recommends oxytocin 10 IU IM or IV as the uterotonic of choice. Ergometrine-containing drugs are avoided in hypertensive women.","why_wrong":{"0":"Ergometrine causes vomiting and hypertension and is not the first-choice prophylactic uterotonic.","1":"Oral misoprostol is recommended only where oxytocin is unavailable.","2":"Carboprost is reserved for treating refractory atonic PPH, not routine prophylaxis.","4":"A 40 IU dose is used as a dilute infusion for treatment of atony; large IV boluses can cause hypotension."}},{"id":65,"subject":"Prenatal and Antenatal Care","topic":"Labour and Puerperium","domain":"knowledge","question":"On day 3 after a spontaneous vaginal delivery, a 26-year-old woman develops a temperature of 39.4°C, severe constant lower abdominal pain out of proportion to the signs, diarrhoea, a pulse of 128/min and hypotension. Her 4-year-old son had a sore throat last week. Which organism is most likely responsible?","options":["Escherichia coli","Staphylococcus aureus","Clostridium perfringens","Streptococcus pyogenes","Bacteroides fragilis"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Group A β-haemolytic streptococcus (S. pyogenes) is the leading cause of death from maternal sepsis (RCOG), often acquired from a child with a sore throat. Red flags include severe pain out of proportion to signs, diarrhoea and vomiting, tachycardia and rapid progression to septic or toxic shock. Management follows the sepsis bundle: cultures, lactate, prompt broad-spectrum IV antibiotics (including clindamycin to block toxin production), fluids and senior review.","why_wrong":{"0":"E. coli commonly causes postpartum urinary infection and pyelonephritis but is not linked to a sore-throat contact.","1":"S. aureus causes wound infection, mastitis and toxic shock, but the household sore throat points elsewhere.","2":"C. perfringens classically causes gas gangrene after septic or unsafe abortion.","4":"Bacteroides is an anaerobe found in polymicrobial endometritis and pelvic abscess, not a fulminant community-acquired picture."}},{"id":66,"subject":"Prenatal and Antenatal Care","topic":"Labour and Puerperium","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 27-year-old woman with a history of bipolar disorder delivered 6 days ago. Her husband reports she has not slept for three nights, is agitated and confused, and says her baby has been replaced by an evil spirit. What is the most appropriate management?","options":["Reassure the family that these are postnatal blues and review in 2 weeks","Start an SSRI and arrange outpatient follow-up","Advise the family to keep the baby away and watch her at home","Arrange counselling with the lady health visitor","Urgent psychiatric admission, ideally to a mother-and-baby unit"],"answer":4,"explanation":"This is puerperal (postpartum) psychosis: about 1–2 per 1000 births, usually starting within the first 2 weeks, with a much higher risk in women with bipolar disorder. It is a psychiatric emergency because of the risk of suicide and infanticide, so urgent admission, ideally to a specialist mother-and-baby unit, is needed. Treatment is with antipsychotics and mood stabilisers, and ECT if severe.","why_wrong":{"0":"Blues occur on days 3–5 with tearfulness and lability but never psychotic features.","1":"An SSRI suits non-psychotic postnatal depression, not an acute psychosis with risk to mother and baby.","2":"Home observation is unsafe given the risks of suicide and infanticide.","3":"Counselling alone is inadequate for a psychiatric emergency."}},{"id":67,"subject":"Prenatal and Antenatal Care","topic":"Labour and Puerperium","domain":"attitude","question":"A 22-year-old primigravida in active labour declines a vaginal examination by a male trainee doctor, saying she feels uncomfortable. Mother and fetus are stable. A female midwife on the ward is trained to perform the examination. What is the most appropriate response?","options":["Proceed with the examination because it is clinically indicated","Ask her husband to persuade her to accept the examination","Respect her refusal and ask the female midwife to examine her","Document that she is uncooperative and stop further assessment","Tell her that declining means she must leave against medical advice"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Respectful maternity care (WHO 2018) means preserving dignity, privacy and informed choice during labour; a competent woman may decline any examination. Her preference for a female examiner should be accommodated where it is safely possible, with the purpose of the examination explained and her decision documented. Examining her without consent would be assault, regardless of clinical indication.","why_wrong":{"0":"Examining a competent woman against her refusal is assault and violates respectful maternity care.","1":"Using family pressure undermines her own free and informed decision.","3":"Labelling her and withdrawing care is disrespectful and unsafe for mother and baby.","4":"Threatening withdrawal of care is coercive and unethical."}},{"id":68,"subject":"Prenatal and Antenatal Care","topic":"Assessment of Fetal Wellbeing","domain":"knowledge","question":"A woman at 40 weeks with pre-eclampsia is in labour. The CTG shows a baseline of 150 bpm, reduced variability and repeated decelerations whose lowest point occurs about 30 seconds after the peak of each contraction, returning to baseline only after the contraction has ended. What is the most likely underlying mechanism?","options":["Fetal head compression causing vagal stimulation","Umbilical cord compression","Uteroplacental insufficiency causing fetal hypoxaemia","A normal fetal sleep cycle","Maternal β-agonist administration"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Late decelerations begin, reach their nadir and recover after the corresponding contraction, reflecting reduced uteroplacental perfusion during contractions that causes fetal hypoxaemia (sensed by chemoreceptors) and, if severe, myocardial depression. They are expected with placental disease such as pre-eclampsia or growth restriction. Repetitive late decelerations with reduced variability are a pathological pattern needing urgent senior review and expedited delivery if not correctable.","why_wrong":{"0":"Head compression produces early decelerations that mirror the contraction and are benign.","1":"Cord compression produces variable decelerations with abrupt falls and recoveries of varying timing.","3":"Fetal sleep reduces variability for up to about 40 minutes but does not cause decelerations.","4":"β-agonists cause fetal tachycardia, not repeated late decelerations."}},{"id":69,"subject":"Prenatal and Antenatal Care","topic":"Assessment of Fetal Wellbeing","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 36-year-old woman at 12+2 weeks' gestation is told that her first-trimester combined screening gives a high chance (1 in 25) of trisomy 21. She wants a definitive diagnosis as early as possible in pregnancy. Which test should be offered?","options":["Chorionic villus sampling","Non-invasive prenatal testing (cell-free DNA)","Amniocentesis","Cordocentesis","Second-trimester quadruple test"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Chorionic villus sampling obtains placental trophoblast for karyotyping from 11 weeks (RCOG: not before 10+0 weeks, because of limb reduction defects), making it the earliest definitive test. Amniocentesis is done from 15+0 weeks, since earlier procedures increase talipes and pregnancy loss. Both carry a small additional miscarriage risk (probably under 0.5%), and CVS can show confined placental mosaicism (about 1%).","why_wrong":{"1":"NIPT is a highly sensitive screening test, not diagnostic; a high-chance result still needs invasive confirmation.","2":"Amniocentesis is performed from 15+0 weeks, so it would delay diagnosis.","3":"Cordocentesis is done from about 18–20 weeks and carries a higher miscarriage risk.","4":"The quadruple test is a screening test at 15–20 weeks, not diagnostic."}},{"id":70,"subject":"Prenatal and Antenatal Care","topic":"Assessment of Fetal Wellbeing","domain":"attitude","question":"Amniocentesis confirms trisomy 21 in the fetus of a 38-year-old woman at 17 weeks' gestation. After balanced counselling about the condition and all available options, she and her husband decide to continue the pregnancy. What is the most appropriate response of the obstetrician?","options":["Strongly advise them to reconsider because of the burden of care","Respect their decision and plan care, including fetal echocardiography","Refer their decision to the hospital ethics committee for review","Discharge her to routine primary care as nothing more can be done","Postpone further discussion until the third trimester"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Prenatal diagnosis counselling must be non-directive: once the couple has made an informed choice, the clinician respects their autonomy and supports them, whatever the decision. About 40–50% of babies with trisomy 21 have congenital heart disease (especially AVSD), so detailed fetal echocardiography and fetal medicine follow-up are appropriate. Neonatal and paediatric planning and links to support groups should also be offered.","why_wrong":{"0":"Directive pressure contradicts the principle of non-directive counselling and disrespects their autonomy.","2":"An informed, lawful choice by competent parents does not need ethics committee review.","3":"She needs ongoing specialist care because of associated cardiac and other anomalies.","4":"Delaying support and planning denies her timely care and information."}},{"id":71,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Benign and Malignant Conditions","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 29-year-old primigravida at 20 weeks' gestation, known to have a 7 cm intramural fibroid, presents with acute localised abdominal pain over the fibroid, low-grade fever and mild leucocytosis. There is no vaginal bleeding and the fetal heart is present. Which change in the fibroid is most likely responsible?","options":["Red (carneous) degeneration","Cystic degeneration","Calcific degeneration","Sarcomatous change","Hyaline degeneration"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Red (carneous) degeneration is haemorrhagic infarction of a fibroid that typically occurs in the second trimester of pregnancy, when rapid growth outstrips its blood supply. It presents with pain and tenderness over the fibroid, low-grade fever and leucocytosis, and is managed conservatively with rest and analgesia.","why_wrong":{"1":"Cystic degeneration follows liquefaction of hyaline areas and is not typically acute or febrile.","2":"Calcific degeneration is seen mainly in postmenopausal women and is asymptomatic.","3":"Sarcomatous change is very rare (<0.5%) and presents with rapid growth, not acute febrile pain in pregnancy.","4":"Hyaline degeneration is the commonest degeneration overall but is usually asymptomatic."}},{"id":72,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Benign and Malignant Conditions","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 16-year-old girl presents with rapidly increasing abdominal distension. Ultrasound shows a 14 cm solid-cystic unilateral ovarian mass. Serum alpha-fetoprotein is markedly raised while hCG and LDH are normal. Histology is most likely to show which feature?","options":["Signet-ring cells","Psammoma bodies","Call-Exner bodies","Rokitansky protuberance with skin adnexa","Schiller-Duval bodies"],"answer":4,"explanation":"A unilateral ovarian mass in a young girl with a markedly raised AFP points to a yolk sac (endodermal sinus) tumour, a malignant germ cell tumour. Its hallmark is the Schiller-Duval body, a glomerulus-like structure with a central vessel. Marker pairings to remember: yolk sac = AFP, dysgerminoma = LDH, choriocarcinoma = hCG, granulosa cell = inhibin, epithelial = CA-125.","why_wrong":{"0":"Signet-ring cells indicate a Krukenberg tumour (metastatic, usually from the stomach).","1":"Psammoma bodies are characteristic of serous epithelial tumours, associated with raised CA-125.","2":"Call-Exner bodies are seen in granulosa cell tumours, whose marker is inhibin.","3":"A Rokitansky protuberance is found in a mature cystic teratoma (dermoid), which does not secrete AFP."}},{"id":73,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Benign and Malignant Conditions","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 56-year-old nulliparous woman with a BMI of 38 kg/m2, type 2 diabetes and a past history of polycystic ovary syndrome presents with postmenopausal bleeding. Endometrial biopsy shows an adenocarcinoma arising against a background of atypical endometrial hyperplasia. Which histological type is most likely?","options":["Endometrioid adenocarcinoma","Clear cell carcinoma","Carcinosarcoma","Serous carcinoma","Leiomyosarcoma"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Obesity, nulliparity, diabetes and PCOS all cause unopposed oestrogen exposure, which leads through atypical hyperplasia to type 1 (endometrioid) endometrial carcinoma. Type 1 tumours are oestrogen-dependent, usually low grade, often carry PTEN mutations and have a good prognosis. Type 2 tumours (serous, clear cell) arise from atrophic endometrium in older, thin women, are linked to p53 mutations and behave aggressively.","why_wrong":{"1":"Clear cell carcinoma is a type 2, oestrogen-independent tumour that does not arise from atypical hyperplasia.","2":"Carcinosarcoma is an aggressive biphasic tumour of older women, not the typical lesion arising from hyperplasia.","3":"Serous carcinoma is a type 2 tumour arising from atrophic endometrium and is not oestrogen-driven.","4":"Leiomyosarcoma is a myometrial smooth-muscle tumour, not an adenocarcinoma."}},{"id":74,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Benign and Malignant Conditions","domain":"knowledge","question":"High-risk human papillomavirus types 16 and 18 cause cervical intraepithelial neoplasia and carcinoma through viral oncoproteins. The E7 oncoprotein promotes uncontrolled cell-cycle progression mainly by binding and inactivating which host protein?","options":["Retinoblastoma protein (pRb)","BRCA1","PTEN","KRAS","p53"],"answer":0,"explanation":"HPV E7 binds and inactivates the retinoblastoma protein, releasing E2F transcription factors and pushing cells from G1 into S phase. E6 binds p53 and targets it for ubiquitin-mediated degradation, removing apoptosis and DNA-repair checkpoints. Mnemonic: 'E7 = Rb, E6 = p53'.","why_wrong":{"1":"BRCA1 mutations are linked to hereditary ovarian and breast cancer, not HPV oncogenesis.","2":"Loss of PTEN is typical of type 1 endometrioid endometrial cancer.","3":"KRAS mutations are seen in mucinous ovarian tumours and some other cancers, and are not the target of HPV E7.","4":"p53 is inactivated by the HPV E6 oncoprotein, not E7."}},{"id":75,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Benign and Malignant Conditions","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 30-year-old woman with long-standing secondary dysmenorrhoea, deep dyspareunia and 3 years of infertility undergoes laparoscopy. A 5 cm left ovarian cyst is found containing thick, dark-brown fluid. Which histological finding in the cyst wall would confirm the diagnosis?","options":["Mucin-secreting columnar epithelium resembling endocervix","Papillary structures with psammoma bodies","Keratinous debris with hair follicles and sebaceous glands","Endometrial glands and stroma with haemosiderin-laden macrophages","Call-Exner bodies within sheets of granulosa cells"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The clinical picture and the 'chocolate' fluid point to an ovarian endometrioma. Histologically, endometriosis is defined by ectopic endometrial glands and stroma, and repeated cyclical haemorrhage leaves haemosiderin-laden macrophages in the cyst wall. Sampson's retrograde menstruation is the most widely accepted theory of how it arises.","why_wrong":{"0":"Mucin-secreting endocervical-type epithelium describes a mucinous cystadenoma.","1":"Papillae with psammoma bodies suggest a serous epithelial tumour.","2":"Keratin, hair and sebaceous glands describe a mature cystic teratoma (dermoid cyst).","4":"Call-Exner bodies indicate a granulosa cell tumour, which produces oestrogen rather than chocolate fluid."}},{"id":76,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Benign and Malignant Conditions","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 52-year-old postmenopausal woman with irregular bleeding has an endometrial biopsy that shows atypical endometrial hyperplasia (endometrial intraepithelial neoplasia). She is medically fit and has completed her family. Which management is most appropriate?","options":["Total hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy","Continuous oral progestogen for 6 months","Levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system","Endometrial ablation","Repeat endometrial biopsy in 12 months"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Atypical hyperplasia carries a high risk of coexisting or future endometrial carcinoma, so RCOG guidance advises total hysterectomy, preferably laparoscopic, with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy in postmenopausal women. Progestogen therapy (LNG-IUS first line) is reserved for hyperplasia without atypia, or for women with atypia who wish to preserve fertility or are unfit for surgery.","why_wrong":{"1":"Oral progestogens are a second-line option for non-atypical hyperplasia and are not definitive for atypia in a fit postmenopausal woman.","2":"LNG-IUS is first line for hyperplasia without atypia, or for atypia only when fertility-sparing or surgery is contraindicated.","3":"Endometrial ablation is not recommended because it does not guarantee complete, durable destruction and it hinders future surveillance.","4":"Surveillance alone is unsafe because of the substantial risk of concurrent carcinoma."}},{"id":77,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Benign and Malignant Conditions","domain":"attitude","question":"A 60-year-old woman is admitted with ascites and a pelvic mass. Biopsy confirms high-grade serous ovarian carcinoma. Before you see her, her son meets you in the corridor and asks you not to tell his mother the diagnosis because 'she will lose hope'. She is fully conscious and has mental capacity. What is the most appropriate response?","options":["Explore the son's concerns, then ask the patient how much she wishes to know and who she wants present","Disclose the full diagnosis and prognosis to the patient immediately, without considering her wishes","Agree with the son and tell the patient she has a benign cyst","Tell the son that withholding information is illegal and refuse to discuss it further","Refer her to oncology without discussing the biopsy result"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A competent adult has the right to information about her own diagnosis, and family members cannot override that. Still, the son's fears deserve to be acknowledged. The best approach is to explore his concerns and then find out from the patient herself how much she wants to know and whom she wants involved. This respects her autonomy without forcing unwanted information on her, in line with the SPIKES model of breaking bad news.","why_wrong":{"1":"Disclosing everything without first establishing what she wants to know ignores her right to control how information is shared.","2":"Lying to a competent patient breaches honesty and her autonomy, and destroys trust.","3":"Confronting the son without engaging his concerns is unprofessional and damages the therapeutic relationship.","4":"Passing her on without informing her avoids the doctor's responsibility and denies her information she may want."}},{"id":78,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Implantation and Early Pregnancy","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 22-year-old woman at 11 weeks' amenorrhoea presents with vaginal bleeding and severe vomiting. The uterus is larger than dates, serum beta-hCG is markedly raised and ultrasound shows a 'snowstorm' appearance with no fetal parts. Histology of the evacuated tissue shows diffuse trophoblastic proliferation and negative p57 immunostaining. What is the most likely karyotype of this tissue?","options":["45,X","69,XXY with two paternal and one maternal haploid sets","46,XX with all chromosomes of paternal origin","46,XX with one maternal and one paternal set","47,XX,+18"],"answer":2,"explanation":"These features describe a complete hydatidiform mole. Most complete moles are 46,XX and androgenetic, formed when one sperm fertilises an ovum without a nucleus and then duplicates. p57 (KIP2) is a paternally imprinted, maternally expressed gene, so its absence confirms that no maternal genome is present. A partial mole is triploid (69,XXY/XXX), contains fetal parts and is p57-positive.","why_wrong":{"0":"Monosomy X is the commonest single chromosomal cause of first-trimester miscarriage, not of molar pregnancy.","1":"Diandric triploidy is the karyotype of a partial mole, which contains fetal tissue and is p57-positive.","3":"A biparental 46,XX karyotype would express p57 and represents a normal or non-molar conception.","4":"Trisomy 18 causes fetal anomalies and growth restriction, not diffuse trophoblastic proliferation without fetal parts."}},{"id":79,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Implantation and Early Pregnancy","domain":"knowledge","question":"A haemodynamically stable 27-year-old woman with mild pelvic pain is diagnosed with an unruptured right tubal ectopic pregnancy. There is no intrauterine pregnancy. Serum hCG is 1,200 IU/L. She is keen on medical treatment. Which additional finding would make systemic methotrexate least appropriate?","options":["Nulliparity","Visible embryonic cardiac activity in the adnexal mass","Adnexal mass measuring 25 mm","A previous ectopic pregnancy treated by salpingectomy","Rhesus-negative blood group"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Methotrexate suits stable women with an unruptured ectopic, no significant pain, a low hCG (NICE: below 1,500 IU/L for first-line use), a mass under 35 mm and no visible heartbeat, who can return for follow-up. Visible embryonic cardiac activity predicts treatment failure and tubal rupture, so surgical management is preferred.","why_wrong":{"0":"Parity has no bearing on whether methotrexate can be used.","2":"A 25 mm mass is within the accepted size limit (under 35 mm) for methotrexate.","3":"A previous ectopic does not contraindicate medical treatment, and it makes conserving the remaining tube more desirable.","4":"Rhesus status does not affect eligibility for methotrexate."}},{"id":80,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Implantation and Early Pregnancy","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 25-year-old woman at 9 weeks' gestation presents with heavy vaginal bleeding and lower abdominal cramps. On speculum examination the internal cervical os is open, but no products of conception have yet been passed. Ultrasound shows a gestational sac low in the uterine cavity. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Threatened miscarriage","Inevitable miscarriage","Incomplete miscarriage","Complete miscarriage","Missed miscarriage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An inevitable miscarriage is bleeding with an open internal os before any products have been passed, so the pregnancy cannot continue. By contrast, threatened miscarriage has a closed os with a viable pregnancy, incomplete miscarriage has an open os after partial passage of products, complete miscarriage has an empty uterus with a closed os, and missed miscarriage is a retained non-viable pregnancy with a closed os.","why_wrong":{"0":"In threatened miscarriage the cervical os remains closed.","2":"Incomplete miscarriage requires some products of conception to have already been passed.","3":"In complete miscarriage all products have been expelled and the os is usually closed.","4":"Missed miscarriage involves a retained non-viable pregnancy with a closed os, often without bleeding."}},{"id":81,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Implantation and Early Pregnancy","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 30-year-old woman was diagnosed with intrauterine fetal death at 30 weeks but declined induction. Five weeks later she presents with bleeding from venepuncture sites and gums. Platelet count is low, fibrinogen is markedly reduced and D-dimer is raised. What is the most likely cause of her bleeding?","options":["Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Vitamin K deficiency","Immune thrombocytopenic purpura","von Willebrand disease"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If a dead fetus is retained for more than about 4 weeks, tissue factor (thromboplastin) released from the fetus and placenta into the maternal circulation can trigger disseminated intravascular coagulation. Clotting factors and platelets are consumed, causing low fibrinogen and platelets with a raised D-dimer. This is why clotting status should be checked and delivery of a retained dead fetus should not be delayed for long.","why_wrong":{"0":"TTP causes microangiopathic haemolysis and thrombocytopenia with characteristically normal coagulation tests.","2":"Vitamin K deficiency prolongs the PT but does not cause thrombocytopenia or hypofibrinogenaemia.","3":"ITP lowers platelets but does not consume fibrinogen or raise D-dimer.","4":"von Willebrand disease causes mucosal bleeding with a normal platelet count and normal fibrinogen."}},{"id":82,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Implantation and Early Pregnancy","domain":"knowledge","question":"Ultrasound at 34 weeks shows a fetus with abdominal circumference below the 3rd centile and head circumference on the 50th centile, giving a raised HC:AC ratio. Liquor is reduced. Which underlying cause is most likely?","options":["Maternal pre-eclampsia","Congenital cytomegalovirus infection","Trisomy 18","First-trimester rubella infection","Turner syndrome"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Asymmetric (head-sparing) growth restriction with oligohydramnios is typical of late uteroplacental insufficiency, as in pre-eclampsia or chronic hypertension. Blood is redistributed preferentially to the brain, while liver glycogen stores and therefore abdominal girth fall. Symmetric growth restriction reflects an early insult such as aneuploidy or congenital infection. Umbilical artery Doppler is the key monitoring tool in suspected placental growth restriction.","why_wrong":{"1":"Congenital CMV affects the fetus early and causes symmetric growth restriction, often with microcephaly.","2":"Trisomy 18 causes early-onset symmetric growth restriction, often with polyhydramnios.","3":"First-trimester rubella causes early symmetric growth restriction with structural anomalies.","4":"Turner syndrome is a chromosomal cause of symmetric growth restriction, often with cystic hygroma or hydrops."}},{"id":83,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Implantation and Early Pregnancy","domain":"attitude","question":"A 28-year-old woman at 36 weeks reports no fetal movements for 24 hours. The consultant's ultrasound confirms absent fetal cardiac activity. She has come alone and, watching your face, asks: 'Is my baby all right?' What is the most appropriate action?","options":["In a private room, tell her clearly and gently that her baby has died, and offer to call her husband or a family member","Begin explaining the induction of labour process before mentioning the scan findings","Ask her to phone her husband so he can be told first and then inform her","Tell her the machine may be faulty and arrange a repeat scan tomorrow","Tell her the baby is probably sleeping and ask her to return if movements do not resume"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When intrauterine death is confirmed and the mother asks directly, she should be told honestly and promptly, in private, using clear words such as 'died' rather than euphemisms. Then allow silence, show empathy and offer to contact her support person. Practical plans for delivery come only after she has had time to take in the news.","why_wrong":{"1":"Discussing induction before breaking the news is confusing and insensitive.","2":"A competent adult is entitled to her own information first, so routing it through her husband breaches her autonomy.","3":"False reassurance when death has already been confirmed is dishonest and leaves her distressed overnight.","4":"Misleading her about the fetal condition is dishonest and delays her care."}},{"id":84,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Obstetric Complications","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 31-year-old G3P2 at 32 weeks presents with a sudden episode of painless, bright red vaginal bleeding of about 200 mL. She is haemodynamically stable. The uterus is soft and non-tender, the fetus is in transverse lie and the CTG is normal. While placental location is being determined, which of the following must be avoided?","options":["Transvaginal ultrasound","Digital vaginal examination","Continuous CTG monitoring","Gentle speculum examination","Intravenous access with cross-match of blood"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Painless bright red bleeding with a soft uterus and malpresentation strongly suggests placenta praevia. Digital vaginal examination must never be done until praevia is excluded, because it can provoke torrential haemorrhage. Transvaginal ultrasound is safe and more accurate than the abdominal route for placental localisation, and a gentle speculum examination can assess the amount and source of bleeding.","why_wrong":{"0":"Transvaginal ultrasound is safe in suspected praevia and is the most accurate way to localise the placenta.","2":"Continuous fetal monitoring is appropriate in antepartum haemorrhage.","3":"A careful speculum examination is acceptable to assess bleeding and exclude local causes.","4":"Wide-bore IV access and cross-matched blood are essential preparations in antepartum haemorrhage."}},{"id":85,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Obstetric Complications","domain":"knowledge","question":"At 20 weeks, a twin pregnancy develops twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome, with polyhydramnios around one twin and anhydramnios around the other. Splitting of the single embryo most likely occurred at which time after fertilisation?","options":["Within 3 days, at the two-cell to morula stage","Between days 4 and 8, at the blastocyst stage","Between days 8 and 12, after amnion formation","After day 13, after formation of the embryonic disc","Two separate ova were fertilised by two sperms"],"answer":1,"explanation":"TTTS develops through unbalanced vascular anastomoses in a shared placenta. It complicates about 10-15% of monochorionic diamniotic twins, which arise when a monozygotic embryo splits between days 4 and 8. Splitting within 3 days gives DCDA twins, splitting at days 8-12 gives MCMA twins, and splitting after day 13 gives conjoined twins.","why_wrong":{"0":"Splitting within the first 3 days produces dichorionic diamniotic twins, each with its own placenta, so TTTS does not occur.","2":"Splitting at days 8-12 produces monoamniotic twins, which can share anastomoses but have no separate sacs to show the classic poly/anhydramnios picture.","3":"Splitting after day 13 results in conjoined twins.","4":"Dizygotic twins are almost always dichorionic, with separate placental circulations, so TTTS does not occur."}},{"id":86,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Obstetric Complications","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 24-year-old woman at 28+2 weeks is in established preterm labour, with the cervix 4 cm dilated. Antenatal corticosteroids have been given. Which additional drug, given before birth, is primarily intended to reduce the baby's risk of cerebral palsy?","options":["Magnesium sulphate","Nifedipine","Erythromycin","Atosiban","Vaginal progesterone"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Intravenous magnesium sulphate given before preterm birth is neuroprotective and reduces the risk of cerebral palsy. NICE advises offering it from 24+0 to 29+6 weeks in established labour or when preterm birth is planned within 24 hours, and considering it up to 33+6 weeks. Antenatal steroids are given separately, mainly to reduce respiratory distress syndrome.","why_wrong":{"1":"Nifedipine is a tocolytic used to delay delivery and has no direct neuroprotective role.","2":"Erythromycin is given in preterm prelabour rupture of membranes to prolong latency and reduce infection.","3":"Atosiban is an oxytocin-receptor antagonist tocolytic, not a neuroprotective agent.","4":"Vaginal progesterone is used to prevent preterm birth in women with a short cervix, not in established labour."}},{"id":87,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Obstetric Complications","domain":"attitude","question":"A 26-year-old woman has a massive postpartum haemorrhage from uterine atony after vaginal delivery and becomes unconscious with a blood pressure of 60/30 mmHg. She had no advance decision or known objection to blood. Her husband refuses blood transfusion because he fears 'infection from other people's blood'. Uterotonics are running. What is the most appropriate action?","options":["Delay transfusion until the hospital ethics committee can meet","Wait until the patient regains consciousness and can give consent","Ask the husband to sign a refusal form and then withhold blood","Transfuse blood in her best interests as emergency life-saving treatment, explaining the reasons to the family","Withhold blood and continue crystalloids only, respecting the husband's refusal"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A relative cannot refuse life-saving treatment on behalf of an incapacitated adult unless there is a valid advance refusal by the patient herself. In an emergency the doctor must act in the patient's best interests, so blood should be transfused while the decision and its safety are explained sensitively to the family. This differs from a competent woman, such as a Jehovah's Witness, whose own informed refusal must be respected.","why_wrong":{"0":"Waiting for an ethics committee in a life-threatening emergency would cost her life.","1":"She may die before regaining consciousness, and the emergency doctrine permits treatment now.","2":"A relative's signature does not make withholding life-saving treatment lawful or ethical.","4":"The husband has no legal authority to refuse life-saving treatment for his adult wife."}},{"id":88,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Diabetes and Hypertension in Pregnancy","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 30-year-old woman at 26 weeks has a 75 g oral glucose tolerance test with these results: fasting 95 mg/dL (5.3 mmol/L), 1-hour 166 mg/dL (9.2 mmol/L) and 2-hour 142 mg/dL (7.9 mmol/L). What is the correct interpretation?","options":["Inconclusive result requiring a repeat OGTT at 32 weeks","Overt (pre-existing) diabetes first detected in pregnancy","Impaired fasting glucose only, not requiring treatment","Normal glucose tolerance","Gestational diabetes mellitus"],"answer":4,"explanation":"By the IADPSG/WHO 2013 criteria, a single value at or above 92 mg/dL fasting (5.1 mmol/L), 180 mg/dL at 1 hour (10.0) or 153 mg/dL at 2 hours (8.5) diagnoses GDM, so a fasting value of 95 mg/dL is enough. The result is also GDM by NICE criteria (fasting ≥5.6 or 2-hour ≥7.8 mmol/L) because of the 2-hour value. Overt diabetes requires a fasting value of at least 126 mg/dL (7.0) or a 2-hour value of at least 200 mg/dL (11.1).","why_wrong":{"0":"A single abnormal value is diagnostic, so there is no indication to repeat and delay treatment.","1":"Overt diabetes needs a fasting value of at least 126 mg/dL or a 2-hour value of at least 200 mg/dL.","2":"In pregnancy an abnormal OGTT value means GDM, which needs management to reduce macrosomia and other complications.","3":"The fasting value exceeds the IADPSG threshold of 92 mg/dL and the 2-hour value exceeds the NICE threshold, so the result is not normal."}},{"id":89,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Diabetes and Hypertension in Pregnancy","domain":"knowledge","question":"A baby born at term to a mother with poorly controlled gestational diabetes becomes jittery 2 hours after birth, with a blood glucose of 1.6 mmol/L (29 mg/dL). What is the underlying mechanism of this hypoglycaemia?","options":["Excess maternal glucagon antibodies crossing the placenta","Fetal pancreatic beta-cell hyperplasia causing hyperinsulinaemia","Transplacental passage of maternal insulin into the fetus","Neonatal adrenal insufficiency due to maternal hyperglycaemia","Deficient hepatic gluconeogenic enzymes from prematurity"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In the Pedersen hypothesis, maternal glucose crosses the placenta freely but insulin does not. The fetus responds to maternal hyperglycaemia with beta-cell hyperplasia and hyperinsulinaemia, which also drives macrosomia. Once the cord is clamped the glucose supply stops abruptly while insulin levels stay high, causing neonatal hypoglycaemia within the first hours.","why_wrong":{"0":"Glucagon antibodies are not a recognised feature of diabetic pregnancy.","2":"Maternal insulin does not cross the placenta in significant amounts.","3":"Maternal diabetes does not cause neonatal adrenal insufficiency.","4":"This is a term infant, and the mechanism in infants of diabetic mothers is hyperinsulinism rather than enzyme immaturity."}},{"id":90,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Diabetes and Hypertension in Pregnancy","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 30-year-old primigravida at 34 weeks presents with epigastric and right upper quadrant pain, nausea and malaise. BP is 152/100 mmHg with 2+ proteinuria. Investigations: Hb 9.4 g/dL with schistocytes on the blood film, platelets 68,000/µL, ALT 290 U/L, LDH 950 U/L, blood glucose 5.2 mmol/L, normal clotting. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Acute fatty liver of pregnancy","Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy","HELLP syndrome","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Acute viral hepatitis E"],"answer":2,"explanation":"HELLP syndrome — Haemolysis (schistocytes, LDH >600 U/L), Elevated Liver enzymes and Low Platelets (<100,000/µL) — is a severe variant of pre-eclampsia, and epigastric/RUQ pain from hepatic swelling is its classic symptom. Management is maternal stabilisation (magnesium sulphate, BP control, correct coagulopathy) and delivery, with steroids for fetal lung maturity if appropriate. Complications include DIC, placental abruption, subcapsular liver haematoma and renal failure.","why_wrong":{"0":"AFLP typically causes hypoglycaemia, coagulopathy, raised ammonia and renal failure, usually without marked haemolysis; her glucose and clotting are normal.","1":"Cholestasis of pregnancy presents with pruritus and raised bile acids, without haemolysis, thrombocytopenia or hypertension.","3":"TTP causes haemolysis and thrombocytopenia but typically with neurological and renal features, and is not accompanied by hypertension, proteinuria and marked transaminitis in this setting.","4":"Viral hepatitis gives very high transaminases (often >1000 U/L) without hypertension, proteinuria or haemolysis."}},{"id":91,"subject":"Pathology in Obstetrics and Gynaecology","topic":"Diabetes and Hypertension in Pregnancy","domain":"knowledge","question":"At her booking visit at 10 weeks, which single risk factor on its own is enough to recommend low-dose aspirin (75-150 mg daily from 12 weeks until birth) to reduce the risk of pre-eclampsia?","options":["Pre-eclampsia in her mother","Chronic hypertension","Body mass index of 32 kg/m2","Maternal age of 38 years","First pregnancy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"NICE (NG133) recommends aspirin 75-150 mg daily from 12 weeks for women with one high-risk factor: hypertensive disease in a previous pregnancy, chronic hypertension, chronic kidney disease, autoimmune disease (SLE or antiphospholipid syndrome), or type 1 or type 2 diabetes. Moderate-risk factors (first pregnancy, age 40 or over, interval over 10 years, BMI 35 or over, family history, multiple pregnancy) justify aspirin only when two or more are present.","why_wrong":{"0":"Family history of pre-eclampsia is a moderate-risk factor and is not enough on its own.","2":"Under NICE, BMI counts as a moderate-risk factor only at 35 kg/m2 or above.","3":"Under NICE, age becomes a moderate-risk factor only at 40 years or above.","4":"Nulliparity is a moderate-risk factor and needs at least one other moderate factor."}},{"id":92,"subject":"Common Congenital and Blood Disorders","topic":"Blood Disorders in Pregnancy","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 24-year-old primigravida at 10 weeks has haemoglobin 10.2 g/dL, MCV 64 fL, normal serum ferritin and HbA2 5.4% on HPLC. She is married to her first cousin. What is the most important next step?","options":["Start intravenous iron sucrose","Arrange a blood transfusion before 12 weeks","Proceed directly to chorionic villus sampling","Reassure her that no further action is needed","Test her husband's carrier status by red cell indices and HPLC"],"answer":4,"explanation":"Microcytosis with normal ferritin and an HbA2 above 3.5% indicates beta-thalassaemia trait. The priority is to screen her partner, especially in a consanguineous marriage. If he is also a carrier, the fetus has a 1 in 4 risk of beta-thalassaemia major, and the couple should be counselled and offered prenatal diagnosis by CVS.","why_wrong":{"0":"Ferritin is normal, so iron is not indicated and could cause iron overload.","1":"A haemoglobin of 10.2 g/dL in a carrier needs no transfusion.","2":"CVS is invasive and is offered only if the partner is also a carrier.","3":"Reassurance misses the chance to identify an at-risk couple and offer prenatal diagnosis."}},{"id":93,"subject":"Common Congenital and Blood Disorders","topic":"Blood Disorders in Pregnancy","domain":"knowledge","question":"A 27-year-old woman at 33 weeks presents with intense itching of her palms and soles, worse at night, without a rash. She is well otherwise, with mildly raised ALT and normal blood pressure. Which investigation is most useful to confirm the likely diagnosis?","options":["Serum alkaline phosphatase","Serum ammonia","Anti-mitochondrial antibodies","Serum total bile acids","Platelet count"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Pruritus of the palms and soles without a rash in the third trimester suggests intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy, and a raised serum bile acid level confirms it. Higher bile acid levels (especially 100 μmol/L or more) are linked to a higher risk of stillbirth and guide the timing of delivery. Ursodeoxycholic acid may relieve itching. The condition resolves after delivery but often recurs in later pregnancies.","why_wrong":{"0":"ALP rises normally in pregnancy because of the placental isoenzyme, so it is not diagnostic.","1":"Serum ammonia is used to assess hepatic encephalopathy, as in acute fatty liver, not cholestasis.","2":"Anti-mitochondrial antibodies diagnose primary biliary cholangitis, which is not the likely condition here.","4":"Low platelets suggest HELLP syndrome, which presents with hypertension and epigastric pain rather than isolated itching."}},{"id":94,"subject":"Infections","topic":"Infections in Pregnancy","domain":"knowledge","question":"A woman who is HBsAg-positive and HBeAg-positive delivers a healthy term baby by spontaneous vaginal delivery. What is the most appropriate management of the newborn to prevent vertical transmission?","options":["Avoid breastfeeding and give formula feeds","Oral lamivudine to the baby for 6 weeks","Check the baby's HBsAg at birth and vaccinate only if negative","Hepatitis B immunoglobulin and hepatitis B vaccine at birth, ideally within 12 hours","Hepatitis B vaccine at 6 weeks with the routine EPI schedule only"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Babies of HBsAg-positive mothers, especially HBeAg-positive ones with high viral load, should receive HBIG (passive) and the first dose of hepatitis B vaccine (active) at different sites within 12 hours of birth, then complete the vaccine schedule. This combined prophylaxis markedly reduces perinatal transmission. Breastfeeding is not contraindicated, and caesarean section is not indicated for HBV alone.","why_wrong":{"0":"Breastfeeding is safe once the baby has received immunoprophylaxis.","1":"Neonatal antivirals are not standard. Antiviral therapy (tenofovir) is given to the mother in the third trimester when viral load is high.","2":"Prophylaxis must not be delayed or made conditional on the baby's serology.","4":"Delaying the first dose to 6 weeks misses the critical perinatal window, when most transmission occurs."}},{"id":95,"subject":"Infections","topic":"Infections in Pregnancy","domain":"attitude","question":"A 25-year-old primigravida at 14 weeks is found to be HIV-positive on antenatal screening. After counselling she agrees to start antiretroviral therapy but begs you not to tell her husband, fearing violence and divorce. What is the most appropriate initial action?","options":["Respect confidentiality for now, counsel her on the benefits of disclosure and offer assisted partner notification, while starting ART","Write the diagnosis on her hand-held antenatal card so the family will find out","Inform the husband immediately without her consent","Inform her mother-in-law so the family can support her","Withhold antenatal care until she agrees to disclose"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Confidentiality is the default, and the first step is supportive counselling about why disclosure protects her partner, with help to disclose (provider-assisted notification) and attention to her safety concerns. Starting ART without delay protects her and the baby. Disclosure without consent is considered only as a last resort, if she persistently refuses and her partner remains at serious, identifiable risk, and she should be told before it happens.","why_wrong":{"1":"Deliberately exposing her status through records is a breach of confidentiality and of trust.","2":"Breaching confidentiality straight away, without counselling or support, is premature and may put her at risk of harm.","3":"Telling other family members has no justification and is a serious breach of confidentiality.","4":"Making care conditional on disclosure is coercive and endangers both mother and baby."}},{"id":96,"subject":"Biostatistics, Research and Biophysics","topic":"Epidemiology and Study Design","domain":"knowledge","question":"The maternal mortality RATIO, as defined by WHO and used in national surveys such as the Pakistan Maternal Mortality Survey 2019, is expressed as the number of maternal deaths per:","options":["1,000 total births","100,000 live births","1,000 live births","100,000 women of reproductive age (15-49 years)","100,000 pregnancies"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The maternal mortality ratio is maternal deaths during a given period per 100,000 live births in the same period, and it measures the obstetric risk of each pregnancy. The maternal mortality rate uses women of reproductive age (15-49) as the denominator, so it reflects both obstetric risk and how often women become pregnant. PMMS 2019 reported Pakistan's MMR as 186 per 100,000 live births.","why_wrong":{"0":"Per 1,000 total births is the denominator for the perinatal mortality rate.","2":"Per 1,000 live births is the denominator for infant or neonatal mortality, not maternal mortality.","3":"Per 100,000 women aged 15-49 is the maternal mortality RATE, not the ratio.","4":"The total number of pregnancies cannot be counted accurately, so live births are used instead."}},{"id":97,"subject":"Biostatistics, Research and Biophysics","topic":"Epidemiology and Study Design","domain":"knowledge","question":"Which of the following correctly defines the perinatal mortality rate as recommended by WHO for international comparison?","options":["Late fetal deaths (28 weeks or more) plus early neonatal deaths (first 7 days) per 1,000 total births","Deaths of infants under 1 year of age per 1,000 live births","Deaths of infants within the first 28 days per 1,000 live births","Early neonatal deaths (first 7 days) per 1,000 live births","Stillbirths plus neonatal deaths within 28 days per 1,000 live births"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The perinatal mortality rate counts late fetal deaths (stillbirths at 28 weeks or more, or 1000 g or more) plus early neonatal deaths in the first week of life. The denominator is total births (live births plus stillbirths) per 1,000. Because stillbirths are in the numerator they must also be in the denominator. The PMR is a sensitive indicator of the quality of antenatal, intrapartum and newborn care. (Park's textbook formula uses live births as the denominator; WHO recommends total births because stillbirths are in the numerator.)","why_wrong":{"1":"Deaths under 1 year per 1,000 live births is the infant mortality rate.","2":"Deaths within 28 days per 1,000 live births is the neonatal mortality rate.","3":"Early neonatal deaths per 1,000 live births is the early neonatal mortality rate, which leaves out stillbirths.","4":"This mixes the perinatal and neonatal periods and uses the wrong denominator."}},{"id":98,"subject":"Biostatistics, Research and Biophysics","topic":"Epidemiology and Study Design","domain":"knowledge","question":"According to the Pakistan Maternal Mortality Survey (PMMS) 2019, what was the leading cause of maternal death in Pakistan?","options":["Hypertensive disorders of pregnancy","Complications of unsafe abortion","Obstetric haemorrhage","Obstructed labour","Puerperal sepsis"],"answer":2,"explanation":"PMMS 2019 identified obstetric haemorrhage, mostly postpartum, as the leading cause of maternal death in Pakistan, followed by hypertensive disorders (pre-eclampsia and eclampsia). This is why active management of the third stage of labour, availability of uterotonics such as oxytocin and misoprostol, and access to blood transfusion are central to Pakistan's maternal health strategy.","why_wrong":{"0":"Hypertensive disorders were the second leading cause in PMMS 2019, after haemorrhage.","1":"Unsafe abortion contributes, but less than haemorrhage.","3":"Obstructed labour contributes, but is not the leading cause.","4":"Sepsis is an important but smaller contributor than haemorrhage."}},{"id":99,"subject":"Biostatistics, Research and Biophysics","topic":"Epidemiology and Study Design","domain":"knowledge","question":"A new first-trimester screening test for pre-eclampsia is evaluated in 1,000 pregnant women, of whom 100 later develop pre-eclampsia. The test is positive in 80 of the women who develop pre-eclampsia and in 90 of the 900 who do not. What is the positive predictive value of the test?","options":["10%","47%","80%","90%","98%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"PPV = true positives / all positives = 80 / (80 + 90) = 80/170 ≈ 47%. The same data give a sensitivity of 80/100 = 80%, a specificity of 810/900 = 90% and an NPV of 810/830 ≈ 98%. PPV falls as disease prevalence falls, which is why screening tests with good sensitivity and specificity can still produce many false positives in low-risk populations.","why_wrong":{"0":"10% is the prevalence of pre-eclampsia in this population (100/1000).","2":"80% is the sensitivity (80/100).","3":"90% is the specificity (810/900).","4":"About 98% is the negative predictive value (810/830)."}},{"id":100,"subject":"Biostatistics, Research and Biophysics","topic":"Epidemiology and Study Design","domain":"knowledge","question":"Researchers identify 150 women who had placental abruption and 300 women who delivered without abruption at the same hospital, then compare how many in each group smoked cigarettes during pregnancy. Which measure of association is most appropriate for this study?","options":["Relative risk","Odds ratio","Incidence rate","Number needed to treat","Prevalence ratio from a single survey"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Groups were selected by outcome (abruption versus none) and then compared for past exposure, so this is a case-control study. Because the investigators fixed the number of cases and controls, incidence cannot be calculated, and the odds ratio is the correct measure of association. When the outcome is rare, the OR approximates the relative risk.","why_wrong":{"0":"Relative risk needs incidence data from a cohort study or RCT, where subjects are grouped by exposure.","2":"Incidence cannot be measured when subjects are selected by outcome status.","3":"NNT comes from intervention trials comparing treatment and control arms.","4":"A prevalence ratio comes from a cross-sectional survey, not a study that selects cases and controls."}}]};
